# The Console Launches (and Re-attaches to) the Intelligence [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

> The launcher harness is what makes an Intelligence's `/compact` SELF-DIRECTED ([CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE]) —
> it owns the child's PTY and types `/compact <direction>` when the instance emits a directive. Today
> starting a harnessed instance is a terminal ritual the human must know and perform, so the DEFAULT
> way an Intelligence runs is UNHARNESSED and therefore unable to compact itself at all. This spec
> makes the Console the entry point: when no instance is connected, the context pane stops showing a
> frozen gauge and shows a Launch affordance; pressing it publishes an intent that the existing
> `app_launcher` turns into a Terminal running the harness. It is "control is a bus peer, not a
> center" applied to INSTANTIATION: a human press and an Intelligence publishing the identical intent
> are indistinguishable downstream — and, because they are, the anti-double-spawn guarantee is placed
> where BOTH paths pass through it.

---

## 1. The loop this closes, and the constraint that shapes it

### [CXC-ICL-THESIS] The harness must be the DEFAULT path, not expert knowledge

- premise: David, 2026-08-10 — "You're not instantiated in the harness that allows you to invoke slash
  commands, like /compact. Seems like the right process would be to launch the Intelligence Console
  and, if it doesn't 'see' a connected instance, for the context pane to change what it shows to a
  'Launch ComposeUX-Architect' button that fires up the launcher app, instantiates a new claude
  instance, and connects the IC to the newly instantiated instance."
- requirement: with NO instance connected, the console's context pane MUST present a launch
  affordance instead of its context gauge, and pressing it MUST result in a harnessed instance —
- rationale: a capability reachable only by a remembered shell ritual is a capability that is not
  taken. The self-directed-`/compact` loop is the single most load-bearing lifecycle behaviour this
  platform has, and it is currently OFF by default because starting it correctly is tacit knowledge.
  Moving the entry point onto the glass converts the harness from expert-knowledge into the default.
- consequence: **(the second failure this closes)** today a dead or never-started instance leaves
  the
  gauge rendering its LAST value. That is the silent-staleness class this system keeps hitting — a
  surface that looks healthy while the thing it measures is absent. The swap is as much an honesty
  fix as a launch feature.
- relates-to: [CXC-ICL-CONSTRAINT-PTY], [CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [CXC-ICL-CONSTRAINT-PTY] The console CANNOT spawn a headless instance — the launcher is a TRANSPARENT proxy and the HUMAN answers the dialogs

- constraint: **(LOAD-BEARING; most other choices in this spec follow from it)**
  `intelligence/launcher/launcher.py` spawns `claude` under `pty.fork()` and then GETS OUT OF THE
  WAY. It forwards the human's terminal to/from the child BYTE-FOR-BYTE, puts its own stdin into raw
  mode, and does exactly three things on top of that wire (actuation relay, harness monitor, session
  pin). The human sees the real Claude Code TUI — including every startup dialog — and answers those
  dialogs THEMSELVES.
- evidence: **(why it is transparent)** an earlier version tried to auto-drive the startup by
  watching PTY
  output and injecting menu keystrokes. In a real run it FAILED — blind injection into Claude Code's
  full-screen TUI startup dialogs COLLIDED WITH THE HUMAN AND HUNG. That is bug #2 in the launcher's
  rebuild notes (`intelligence/launcher/README.md`, "Why this design (a live shakedown)"), and the
  transparent proxy is the fix, not an accident.
- requirement: **(therefore)** a console-initiated launch MUST spawn a **TERMINAL WINDOW** running
  the
  harness entry script, so that a real human terminal exists for the launcher to proxy. It MUST NOT
  spawn `claude` (or `launcher.py`) as a detached background subprocess with `stdout/stderr` to
  `DEVNULL` — that is the current `app_launcher._spawn_argv` idiom and it would produce an instance
  sitting on an unanswerable dialog with nobody attached, i.e. a launch that silently never completes.
- forbidden: **(permanently)** auto-answering startup dialogs by keystroke injection, in any form,
  under any
  flag. Re-proposing it re-opens a defect that was already paid for on the glass.
- not-implied-by-the-above: **(the correction, David 2026-08-10)** the transparent-proxy constraint
  bans
  auto-answering DIALOGS. It does NOT ban the launcher's re-register BOOTSTRAP — a prompt typed into
  the instance's ordinary entry area AFTER startup settles. The bootstrap works and is now the DEFAULT
  for a console-initiated cold start; see [CXC-ICL-COLD]. These two were conflated in `launcher.py`'s
  own prose, which is why that prose is corrected as part of this build.
- upholds: [CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [CXC-ICL-SCOPE-BOUNDARY] This is the PRIMARY-instance launch, not the fleet spawner — and the three do not overlap

- definition: three launch-shaped things now exist in this corpus and a builder WILL confuse them.
  This node is the disambiguation, stated once.

| Surface | What it does | Mode | Owner |
|---------|--------------|------|-------|
| [CXC-IH-HOST] `IntelligenceHost` | spawns N parallel PEER intelligences (a builder alongside the architect) | **headless only** — `spawn()` RAISES `NotImplementedError` for `mode="interactive"`, with the message "interactive spawn is deferred to launcher.Launcher (PTY)" | `intelligence/host/host.py` |
| [CXC-LB-ROSTER] tile 2 `_app/harness/open` | FOCUSES the already-running harness; ratified as a `HOST` kind, never spawns (David 2026-07-29) | n/a | `app_launcher` TARGET_TABLE |
| **THIS spec** `_app/intelligence/open` | brings the PRIMARY instance INTO EXISTENCE when none is connected | **interactive** — a Terminal + the PTY launcher | `app_launcher` TARGET_TABLE, one new row |
- consequence: `IntelligenceHost` is not a candidate consumer for this intent — it structurally cannot
  serve it, and the refusal is in its own code, not an oversight. `app_launcher` is therefore the ONE
  spawner ([DD-LB-LAUNCH-MECHANISM] holds unchanged, ratified again by David 2026-08-10: "the button
  has to spawn a terminal running transport.sh — reuse what's present") and gains exactly one row.
- constraint: this spec MUST NOT modify `_app/harness/open`'s HOST/focus semantics. A ratified decision
  is not silently re-interpreted because a new need arrived; the new need gets a new key
  ([DD-ICL-INTENT-KEY]).
- relates-to: [CXC-IH-CONSOLE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

---

## 2. Presence — reuse the ONE computation, mint no new liveness

### [CXC-ICL-PRESENCE] Presence is TWO signals — `channel/alive` proves REACHABLE, `self/daemons` proves HARNESSED, and only both together mean CONNECTED

- correction: **(David, 2026-08-10, verified live on this machine — this inverts the original
  brief)**
  `channel/alive` freshness alone is NOT sufficient presence. Observed simultaneously, on the very
  session that raised this:

  | Signal | Value | Means |
  |--------|-------|-------|
  | `intelligence/architect/channel/alive` | PRESENT, fresh | an Intelligence is REACHABLE |
  | `self/daemons[intelligence_launcher].status` | `down` (no process, flock unheld, recorded pid 82381 dead) | it is NOT HARNESSED |

  That combination is a bare `claude` with the `composeio-channel` MCP loaded and NO launcher: it
  publishes a perfect heartbeat and it CANNOT self-`/compact`, because no launcher owns its PTY to
  type the command. The missing capability is the entire reason this feature exists.
- consequence: **(the defect this node prevents)** a console that hides the Launch affordance
  whenever
  `channel/alive` is fresh would auto-connect to a session like that one, hide the button, and leave
  the human with an Intelligence that silently cannot compact itself. The affordance would never
  appear in exactly the situation it was built for — a one-signal design is not merely incomplete, it
  is inverted.
- definition: **(signal 1 — REACHABLE)** `intelligence/<id>/channel/alive`, published by
  `channel.mjs`
  (the MCP channel server running INSIDE the instance, ~5 s cadence) and relayed onto the bus by
  `channel_bridge`. Live payload, observed 2026-08-10:
  `{"ts": 1786382845886, "id": "architect", "pid": 83035, "start": 1786214825852, "delivery": "ok", "unacked": 1}`.
  It is ALREADY reduced to a verdict by `python/composeio/render/widgets/link_health.py` —
  arrival-stamped freshness (`time.monotonic()` per beat, immune to clock skew) against
  `FRESH_THRESHOLD_S = 12.0` (~2 missed beats, so one dropped beat does not flip it), combined with
  the transport's `is_connected` — and PUBLISHED to `console/link/state` as
  `engaged` / `channel_offline` / `bus_offline`, plus the THIRD value `degraded` that
  [DD-ICL-DEGRADED] adds (see the requirement below).
- definition: **(signal 2 — HARNESSED)** `self/daemons`, published by
  `intelligence/publisher/daemon_watch.py` every 5 s at global scope, carrying an entry per daemon:
  `{"<daemon-id>": {"status": "up"|"down"|"duplicate", "pids": [...], "flock_pid": N, "flock_held": bool, ...}}`.
  The entry of interest is `intelligence_launcher`, whose registered probe is
  `DaemonSpec("intelligence_launcher", "intelligence-launcher-{id}", "launcher/launcher.py")` — the
  flock holder UNIONED with a process scan, exactly the two ways a launcher can be present.
  `status == "up"` means HARNESSED.
- requirement: **(mint nothing)** this spec MUST NOT create a liveness namespace, a presence daemon,
  a
  harness prober, or a second freshness timer. Both signals exist, are live, and are already on the
  bus. The console READS them.
- requirement: **(two signals is NOT a fork — do not "de-fork" them)** [DX-NARROW-WAIST] forbids a
  SECOND
  computation of the SAME fact. Reachability and harnessing are DIFFERENT facts with different
  sources, so reading both is correct. What remains forbidden is re-deriving reachability: the panel
  MUST take reachability from `console/link/state` and MUST NOT subscribe `channel/alive` for a
  freshness verdict of its own. (It may read the alive payload for the cosmetic last-seen line,
  [CXC-ICL-PANEL] — a render of the last value, not a computation.)
- requirement: **(the scene must DECLARE `self/daemons` — it does not today)**
  `console.compose.yaml`
  carries NO `self/daemons` namespace declaration. It MUST gain one
  (`{name: self/daemons, state_engine: json, export: true, scope: global, label: "Daemon liveness"}`),
  or the panel's subscription is inert and the harness signal silently never arrives — which would
  land the pane permanently in the "harness unknown" rendering below.
- default: **(harness staleness)** a `self/daemons` older than `DAEMONS_FRESH_S` (**default 15.0 s**
  — 3 x
  the 5 s publish cadence, one more beat of tolerance than the channel's 2, because a slower publisher
  deserves a wider window) is UNKNOWN, not `down`.
- requirement: **(UNKNOWN is its own answer, and it renders the GAUGE with a marker)** when the
  harness
  signal is stale or absent while reachability is `engaged`, the pane shows the LIVE GAUGE plus an
  explicit `harness: unknown` marker. It does NOT show the warning pane and it does NOT hide the
  gauge. Rationale: treating unknown as UNHARNESSED would replace a good gauge with a false alarm
  every time `daemon_watch` blips; treating it as HARNESSED silently would restore the original
  defect. Preserve-and-flag is this corpus's answer to exactly this shape, and it is the only option
  that is neither a false alarm nor a silent guess.
- known-hazard: **(the watcher's `episodic` flag must NOT be inherited)** `daemon_watch` marks
  `intelligence_launcher` as `episodic=True`, whose comment reads "it runs ONLY while a Claude session
  is hosted under the harness, so 'down' is its normal resting state, not a defect." That is correct
  FOR THE WATCHER — it suppresses a staleness canary so the defect sink does not cry wolf. The CONSOLE
  MUST NOT inherit that reading. Under this spec, `intelligence_launcher == down` WHILE the channel is
  alive is precisely the anomaly worth surfacing. Same fact, two consumers, two correct
  interpretations; a builder who reuses the watcher's "down is fine" logic here reproduces the defect.
- default: **(headless)** `link_health.compute_state` returns `engaged` when `connected is None` —
  no
  external bus transport, i.e. a headless render or a local-only demo window. With no `self/daemons`
  either, the harness signal is UNKNOWN, so a headless render shows the gauge (plus the marker, which
  a capture test pins). Every existing capture stays byte-identical except for that marker's addition,
  which is re-baselined ONCE and reviewed ([CXC-ICL-ACCEPT] ICL-C6).
- requirement: **(DEGRADED delivery IS surfaced — a THIRD VERDICT VALUE, computed ONCE;
  [DD-ICL-DEGRADED] RESOLVED by David 2026-08-10, reversing the deferral)** `delivery != "ok"` or a
  climbing `unacked`
  means the channel is up but NOT DELIVERING. `link_health` MUST read them and FOLD them into the
  SINGLE verdict it already publishes, so `console/link/state` takes the values
  `engaged` / `degraded` / `channel_offline` / `bus_offline`. EVERY consumer — the header dot, the
  header `link_label`, the composer's `composer_send_label`, and the launch panel — reads that ONE
  value (the full consumer list matters: see the `map:`-entry requirement below, which is where this
  slice most plausibly ships broken). The panel MUST NOT derive `degraded` from the alive payload
  itself; a second derivation lets the dot and the pane disagree with no way for a human to tell which
  is lying, and the ONE-computation rule stated above admits no exception for this fact.
- requirement: **(what `degraded` means for the four states — RATIFIED, composeux-architect
  2026-08-10; the previous pass PROPOSED this and flagged it, this is that ratify)** `degraded` is a
  REACHABLE-side
  value — the channel is up, and a reachable, harnessed instance therefore still EXISTS — so it
  resolves as `reachable = yes` in [CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY]'s truth table. It consequently NEVER withholds
  the gauge and NEVER reveals the launch button: offering to launch a SECOND instance because the
  first one's channel is slow would be actively wrong, and it is `absent` / `unharnessed`, not this,
  that the button exists for. It renders as a MARKER on the state already shown, by exact analogy to
  the `harness: unknown` marker, so NO fifth row is added to [CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY]'s table.
- requirement: **(state PRECEDENCE — one ordered cascade, stated so it is not derived)** the verdict
  is
  computed as `bus_offline` > `channel_offline` > `degraded` > `engaged`. Offline BEATS degraded: an
  instance whose heartbeat has gone stale is not "degraded", it is GONE, and a degraded reading over a
  dead channel would be a slower, less honest version of the frozen gauge this spec exists to remove.
- requirement: **(WHERE the marker renders — two surfaces, and no third derivation)** (a) the HEADER
  carries it ALWAYS — the `link_health` dot plus the `link_label` beside it, which are on screen in
  every state, connected or not; (b) the `intelligence_card` carries a `channel: degraded` marker
  whenever the GAUGE is the shown surface, as a bound label reading `console/link/state` DIRECTLY (an
  attribute `map:` binding, the same idiom `link_label` already uses in the same scene) — no new
  namespace and no `console/instance/harness`-style derived channel, because the verdict is already
  published. The `instance_launch_panel` needs NO `degraded` rendering at all: its ONLY degraded
  knowledge is `reachable = yes`, which is the truth-table rule above.
- requirement: **(EVERY `map:` consumer of `console/link/state` gains a `degraded` entry — this is
  the most likely way this slice ships broken)** besides the dot that publishes it,
  `console/link/state` has
  bound consumers in `console.compose.yaml` TODAY — the header `link_label` (text + colour) and the
  composer's `composer_send_label` (text + colour) — and an attribute `map:` with no key for the
  arriving value falls back to its `default:`, which for BOTH of them is the HEALTHY look ("ENGAGED"
  green, "SEND" dark ink). A third verdict value with no map entries would therefore render a degraded
  channel as a green ENGAGED header: exactly the silent-staleness lie this spec exists to remove,
  reintroduced by omission. Every `map:` binding on this namespace MUST gain a `degraded` key in the
  SAME commit, and [CXC-ICL-ACCEPT] ICL-C19 leg (b) is the mechanical guard.
- defaults: **(the `degraded` RENDERING, stated so nothing is guessed; PROVISIONAL pending David's
  glass)**
  - the dot: `theme.warn` (amber) and NOT beating — it falls into `link_health`'s EXISTING non-engaged
    branch, so the change is one more `elif`, not a new visual mode. It shares amber with `bus_offline`
    deliberately; the `link_label` beside it is what NAMES which one, exactly as `channel_offline` and
    `bus_offline` already share the not-beating rest state.
  - `link_label`: text `DEGRADED`, colour amber `0xF5 0xA6 0x23` — the SAME amber `bus_offline`
    already uses in that map, not a new ink
  - `composer_send_label`: text `DEGRADED`, colour amber — David sees that a send may not land AS he
    types it, which is the failure that label was added for in the first place
  - the `intelligence_card` marker: `channel: degraded — messages may not be arriving`
- defaults: **(the `degraded` THRESHOLDS — concrete enough to build, and explicitly TUNABLE on the
  glass; PROVISIONAL)** computed in `link_health` and NOWHERE else ([DD-ICL-DEGRADED]'s binding
  constraint).
  - `degraded` is TRUE when EITHER (i) the latest `channel/alive` payload has `delivery != "ok"`, OR
    (ii) `unacked` is STRICTLY INCREASING across `DEGRADED_CLIMB_N` (**default 3**) consecutive alive
    payloads.
  - it returns to normal only after `DEGRADED_RECOVER_N` (**default 1**) full payload carrying
    `delivery == "ok"` AND a non-increasing `unacked` — deliberate HYSTERESIS, so a single blip cannot
    flap the header dot, which is the most-watched pixel in this console.
  - both runs count PAYLOAD ARRIVALS, not seconds. A channel that stops publishing therefore cannot
    recover out of `degraded` by going silent; it ages into `channel_offline` by freshness instead,
    per the precedence cascade above.
  - a payload with NO `delivery` key reads as `"ok"` (an absent field is not evidence of failure); a
    payload with no `unacked` key RESETS the climb run (no evidence either way); any non-increasing
    `unacked` observation resets the run to zero; and the first payload after mount has no predecessor,
    so it can never itself be a climb.
- known-hazard: **(these numbers are DEFENSIBLE, not MEASURED — and the retuning observation is
  named)**
  3-and-1 is chosen by symmetry with `FRESH_THRESHOLD_S`'s "~2 missed beats" reasoning, NOT from a
  measured distribution of `unacked` on a healthy channel, because nobody has watched one. The retune
  signals are stated so the glass gate can settle them ([CXC-ICL-ACCEPT] ICL-C20): if David sees the
  header amber while the Intelligence is demonstrably receiving — a reply arrives to a message sent
  INSIDE the amber window — the trigger is TOO EAGER, and `DEGRADED_CLIMB_N` rises or the set of
  `delivery` values that count narrows. If he hits another "looked sent, never seen" episode while the
  header stayed green, it is TOO LAX, and it falls or gains an absolute `unacked` ceiling. Retuning a
  NUMBER is in scope for that gate; widening a threshold to silence a FLAP is not — that is the retreat
  [DD-ICL-DEGRADED]'s invalidating assumption forbids, whose correct answer is its alternative (b).
- verify-machine: a test asserts the panel subscribes BOTH `console/link/state` and `self/daemons`;
  that it contains no second freshness constant for reachability and never reads `delivery`/`unacked`
  itself (the `degraded` verdict arrives ONLY via `console/link/state`); and a truth-table test driving
  all four combinations of (reachable, harnessed) plus both unknown cases and the `degraded`
  reachability value, asserting the [CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY] rendering for each — including that a state-2
  launch intent carries the recovery id ([DD-ICL-UNHARNESSED-ACTION]), never the canonical one.
- relates-to: [CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY] ONE rule — the gauge shows when CONNECTED, and connected means BOTH signals; the four states are named

- decision: **(David 2026-08-10)** the rule is unchanged — "the button is visible only when no
  instance is
  connected" — but CONNECTED now means REACHABLE **and** HARNESSED ([CXC-ICL-PRESENCE]). The four
  combinations are named, and each has one rendering. There is still no `launching` state
  ([DD-ICL-NO-LAUNCHING-STATE]).

| # | State | reachable | harnessed | The pane shows | The button |
|---|-------|-----------|-----------|----------------|-----------|
| 1 | `live` | yes | yes | the `intelligence_card` context gauge — EXACTLY as today | not present |
| 2 | `unharnessed` | yes | **no** | a WARNING card: "reachable but NOT harnessed — this Intelligence cannot self-compact" and, as the offered action, a RECOVERY launch that leaves the running session alone | present, enabled, labeled "Launch recovery instance" — it publishes the intent under the DISTINCT recovery id `recovery-architect-2`, never the canonical id ([DD-ICL-UNHARNESSED-ACTION], RESOLVED David 2026-08-10) |
| 3 | `orphaned` | no | yes | a WARNING card: "a launcher is running for this id but its channel is silent — check its Terminal window" | present but **DISABLED** |
| 4 | `absent` | no | no | the launch card | present, enabled, "Launch ComposeUX-Architect" |
| — | `harness_unknown` | yes | UNKNOWN | the gauge, plus a `harness: unknown` marker ([CXC-ICL-PRESENCE]) | not present |
| — | `bus_unreachable` | `bus_offline` | any | the launch card body reading "BUS OFFLINE — cannot reach the launcher" | present but **DISABLED** |

- requirement: **(`degraded` adds NO row to this table)** the third verdict value `degraded`
  resolves as
  `reachable = yes` here and changes nothing else — states 1-4 are selected EXACTLY as if the verdict
  read `engaged`, so a degraded+harnessed instance is state 1 `live` (the gauge, no button) and a
  degraded+unharnessed one is state 2. The condition is carried by the header and by a marker on
  whichever surface is already shown ([CXC-ICL-PRESENCE]), never by a fifth state and never by a fifth
  row. A builder who adds one has coupled a DELIVERY fault to a PRESENCE decision, which is how the
  launch button appears beside a perfectly live Intelligence.
- requirement: **(state 2 is NOT healthy and MUST say so)** `unharnessed` is the state that
  motivated this
  correction. It is the ONLY state where everything looks fine — a live gauge would render, the header
  dot beats green, messages send and arrive — while the instance silently lacks the one capability the
  harness exists to provide. The pane MUST name the loss in words ("cannot self-compact"), not merely
  withhold the gauge, because a human with no gauge would reasonably conclude the instance is dead
  rather than crippled.
- requirement: **(state 3 DISABLES the button — a launch there cannot succeed)** with a launcher
  already
  holding `intelligence-launcher-<id>`, a second launcher YIELDS and exits ([CXC-ICL-SINGLETON-YIELD]).
  Offering an enabled button that will provably do nothing is the same lie as offering one while the
  bus is down. The disabled body names where to look instead.
- requirement: **(no frozen gauge, ever)** in states 2, 3 and 4 the `intelligence_card` MUST NOT be
  drawn
  and MUST NOT occupy layout space. Leaving a dimmed gauge alongside the warning reproduces exactly the
  stale-value failure this spec exists to remove.
- requirement: **(there is NO `launching` STATE)** the window between the press and the first
  `channel/alive` beat is real and can be a minute or more — the Terminal is opening, Claude Code is
  starting, and the human is answering startup dialogs at human pace. Through all of it the instance is
  not connected, so BY THIS RULE THE BUTTON REMAINS ON SCREEN AND REMAINS PRESSABLE. That is
  deliberate. A second press is a HARMLESS NO-OP because the anti-double-spawn guarantee lives in the
  consuming daemon ([CXC-ICL-SPAWN-ONCE]), not in the widget.
- rationale: **(why the guard is NOT a UI state — this is the load-bearing part)** a UI latch would
  protect
  the human path and leave the BUS path unguarded, because an Intelligence publishing
  `_app/intelligence/open` never consults the widget. That asymmetry is precisely what
  control-is-a-peer exists to prevent ([CXC-LB-CREDO]). Putting spawn-once in the daemon means a
  double-click and a double-publish get the identical dedupe, and the widget stays a pure intent
  emitter with no lifecycle knowledge.
- requirement: **(the `bus_offline` variation is a RENDERING, not a state)** when
  `console/link/state == bus_offline` the intent physically cannot reach `app_launcher` — it is an
  out-of-process daemon reachable only over the global bus ([CXC-LB-RIBBON-BUS-BOUND] is the same
  class: a publish that never leaves the process). The panel therefore renders its button DISABLED with
  the body reading "BUS OFFLINE — cannot reach the launcher". This is expressed as an attribute
  `map:` binding on the already-published `console/link/state` — the identical idiom the header's
  `link_label` already uses in the same scene — so it adds NO branch, no timer, and no second state
  machine. A button that cannot work must not look pressable; that is honesty, not a state.
- scenario:
  - when: the instance exits and `channel/alive` stops
  - then: within `FRESH_THRESHOLD_S` (12 s) the gauge is REPLACED by the launch panel — not frozen,
    not dimmed
- scenario:
  - when: a bare `claude` runs with the channel MCP loaded but no launcher, so `channel/alive` is fresh
    and `self/daemons[intelligence_launcher].status` is `down`
  - then: the pane shows the `unharnessed` WARNING naming the lost self-compact capability — it does
    NOT show a healthy gauge
- scenario:
  - when: the human presses the button and, 20 s later, presses it again while still answering dialogs
  - then: the button was on screen and accepted both presses; exactly ONE Terminal exists
    ([CXC-ICL-SPAWN-ONCE])
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

---

## 3. The pane swap — an existing engine primitive, no new widget seam

### [CXC-ICL-PANE-SWAP] The swap rides `ordered_stack`'s `visibility_namespace` — the ONE mechanism that removes a child from FLOW

- definition: the collaboration rail in `intelligence/console/console.compose.yaml` is ALREADY an
  `ordered_stack` ([SPEC:CXC-ORDER]) carrying `state_namespace: _app/console/collab_order`. That
  container's `_flow_children()` excludes hidden ids from LAYOUT, so a hidden card leaves the flow, the
  rail reflows, and nothing is drawn or hit-tested.
- requirement: the rail gains ONE key — `visibility_namespace: console/instance/pane` — and the launch
  panel is declared as a rail child immediately after `intelligence_panel`. The shown-map published on
  `console/instance/pane` is `{"intelligence_panel": <bool>, "instance_launch_panel": <bool>}`, exactly
  one of which is true.
- requirement: **(why NOT a plain `visible:` binding)** `visible:` bindings exist ([CXC-RM-M4]
  engine-addition #2, `visible: {namespace:, when:, invert:, default:}`) and DO work — but a plain
  `FlexContainer._flow_children()` returns the declared child list regardless of the `visible` flag, so
  a `visible: false` card still CONSUMES ITS MEASURED HEIGHT and leaves a hole in the rail. The
  `visibility_namespace` path is the one that reflows. A builder who reaches for `visible:` here will
  ship a 300px gap.
- requirement: **(why NOT a fixed-height `pixel_stack` overlay)** `IntelligenceCard.measure` is
  DYNAMIC —
  its height is `HEAD_H + MEM_H + GAUGE_H + 3*ROW_H + reg_extra + SUB_CAP_H + chips_h + PAD_BOTTOM`,
  where `reg_extra` and `chips_h` vary with the expanded registry and the live subagent chips. Any
  fixed-height slot would clip it. Rejected for that reason, recorded so it is not re-proposed.
- requirement: **(`visibility_namespace` is a SEPARATE source — no clobber)** the shown-map MUST go
  on its
  own namespace, NOT into `_app/console/collab_order`'s `{hidden: [...]}` key. `state_namespace` is the
  human/Intelligence-drivable rail REORDER channel; a presence publisher writing full state there would
  clobber any concurrent reorder, and vice versa. The engine already provides the two independent
  sources; use both.
- requirement: **(the declared namespace + its `initial:`)** `console/instance/pane` is declared in
  the
  scene's `namespaces:` block as `{state_engine: json, export: true, scope: local, initial:
  {intelligence_panel: true, instance_launch_panel: false}}`. `scope: local` because the swap decision
  is computed in-console and no out-of-process peer drives it. The `initial:` is LOAD-BEARING: without
  it the launch panel is shown-by-default at boot and occupies rail space before any publish, which
  breaks the no-regression bar ([CXC-ICL-ACCEPT] ICL-C6).
- constraint: **(zero engine change)** no new widget primitive, no parser change, no new binding
  kind. If a
  builder finds they need one, that is a signal the design drifted — stop and re-ratify.
- finding: **(spec gap, flagged for ratify — NOT a blocker)** `[SPEC:CXC-ORDER]` is cited by
  `layout.py`, `parser.py`, `console.compose.yaml` and `test_ordered_container.py`, but NO `.lts`
  document carrying that id exists in this corpus. The primitive is real, tested, and in production;
  its SPEC is a docstring. A builder following the reference will find nothing. This spec depends on
  that primitive, so the gap is named here rather than inherited silently — authoring the missing
  document is a separate, small task ([DD-ICL-PANE-OWNER] is unaffected either way).
- verify-machine: a scene test asserts the rail declares `visibility_namespace: console/instance/pane`,
  that `console/instance/pane` is declared with the stated `initial:`, and that publishing
  `{"intelligence_panel": false, "instance_launch_panel": true}` removes the card from
  `_flow_children()` AND collapses its layout height to zero (not merely stops drawing it).
- uses: [SPEC:CXC-ORDER]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [CXC-ICL-PANEL] The launch panel — a rail card that owns the swap and the press, and NOTHING about lifecycle

- definition: a new widget `instance_launch_panel`
  (`python/composeio/render/widgets/instance_launch_panel.py`), a rail card in the console-theme card
  idiom, sized `width: 332` to match the rail.
- requirement: **(it owns the shown-map — ONE publisher)** the panel subscribes BOTH
  `console/link/state`
  and `self/daemons`, resolves [CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY]'s state from the two, and publishes the shown-map
  to `console/instance/pane` ON CHANGE ONLY. It hides ITSELF in `live` and `harness_unknown` and
  reveals the card. Exactly one widget writes that namespace; a second writer is a review reject
  ([DX-LEAF-OWNS-BEHAVIOR] — the leaf owns its behaviour).
- requirement: **(the harness marker is the card's, not the panel's)** in `harness_unknown` the
  panel is
  HIDDEN, so the `harness: unknown` marker cannot be drawn by it. The marker is a small bound label on
  the `intelligence_card` side, driven by a derived namespace `console/instance/harness` (values
  `up` / `down` / `unknown`) that the panel publishes alongside the shown-map. One publisher, two
  consumers — the panel and the card — and no second derivation.
- requirement: **(press publishes and STOPS)** on press the panel publishes the launch intent
  ([CXC-ICL-INTENT]) and nothing else. It does not spawn, does not block, does not await the spawn, and
  does NOT latch, disable, or time itself out. This is the widget-owns-intent / launcher-owns-runtime
  split every existing launcher enforces ([CXC-LB-CREDO], [CXC-LB-INTENT-BINDING]), and it is why the
  panel carries no lifecycle logic at all.
- requirement: **(the button's enabled-ness and body follow the STATE, by a bound map)** `enabled`
  and the
  body text bind to the panel's own resolved state (`unharnessed` / `orphaned` / `absent` /
  `bus_unreachable`) via attribute `map:` bindings, exactly as the header `link_label` in the same
  scene binds text+colour to `console/link/state`. Enabled in `unharnessed` and `absent`; DISABLED in
  `orphaned` and `bus_unreachable` ([CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY]). No conditional code path beyond the state
  resolution itself.
- requirement: **(`degraded` is READ as reachable, and rendered nowhere here)** in the panel's state
  resolution the verdict value `degraded` is treated EXACTLY as `engaged` — `reachable = yes` — so the
  panel keeps hiding itself in `live` and `harness_unknown` and the launch button is never revealed by
  a delivery fault ([CXC-ICL-PRESENCE], [CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY]). The panel adds NO `degraded` body, NO
  `degraded` marker and NO `degraded` branch: the condition is carried by the header (always on screen)
  and by the `intelligence_card`'s marker (when the gauge is the shown surface). A `degraded` string
  appearing anywhere in this module other than the reachable-mapping is a review reject.
- defaults: **(the four bodies, stated so they are not invented)**
  - `absent` — "No Intelligence is running." / button "Launch ComposeUX-Architect"
  - `unharnessed` — "Reachable, but NOT harnessed — this Intelligence cannot self-compact. Launch a
    recovery instance (`recovery-architect-2`); the running session is left untouched." / button
    "Launch recovery instance" ([DD-ICL-UNHARNESSED-ACTION] — this is a RECOVERY/fallback mode, and the
    body MUST NOT instruct the human to exit anything; the distinct id is what makes the second
    instance safe)
  - `orphaned` — "A launcher is running for this id but its channel is silent. Check its Terminal
    window." / button disabled
  - `bus_unreachable` — "BUS OFFLINE — cannot reach the launcher." / button disabled
- requirement: **(the last-seen line — honesty about what is gone)** when a `channel/alive` payload
  was
  seen this session the panel renders the identity it last saw: `pid` and a human-readable age since
  `start` (e.g. "last seen: pid 83035, up 46h"). With none ever seen it renders "no instance has
  connected this session". It MUST NOT render a context percentage — the last known context level is
  exactly the stale number this spec removes. (This is a RENDER of the last payload the panel received;
  it is not a freshness computation — the verdict still comes from `console/link/state`,
  [CXC-ICL-PRESENCE].)
- requirement: **(COSMETIC ONLY ([DD-ICL-LAUNCH-HINT], RESOLVED David 2026-08-10 — it ships))** for
  `LAUNCH_HINT_S`
  (**default 90.0 s**) after this panel last published an intent, its sub-line reads
  "launch requested — answer the dialogs in the Terminal window" instead of the last-seen line. This
  changes NO behaviour: the button stays enabled, a press still publishes, and the daemon still
  dedupes. It exists only so the human is not left wondering whether the press registered. It is a
  label, not a state — it has no effect on the shown-map, on `enabled`, or on the intent.
- defaults: **(every value stated, so nothing is guessed)**
  - `link_state_namespace` = `console/link/state`
  - `pane_namespace` = `console/instance/pane`
  - `intent_namespace` = `_app/intelligence/open`
  - `alive_namespace` = `intelligence/architect/channel/alive` (READ-ONLY, for the last-seen line only)
  - `intelligence_id` = `architect` (the scene passes it; it is CARRIED, never guessed —
    [CXC-IC-SELF-IDENTITY])
  - `label` = `Launch ComposeUX-Architect`
  - `width` = `332`; height = natural measure (the rail reflows; it is never asked to match the gauge
    card's height)
  - `LAUNCH_HINT_S` = `90.0`
- scenario:
  - when: `console/link/state` publishes `channel_offline`
  - then: the panel publishes `{"intelligence_panel": false, "instance_launch_panel": true}` and the
    rail reflows with the launch card in the gauge card's place
- scenario:
  - when: `console/link/state` returns to `engaged`
  - then: the panel publishes `{"intelligence_panel": true, "instance_launch_panel": false}` and the
    live gauge returns — with NO console bounce
- scenario:
  - when: `console/link/state` publishes `bus_offline`
  - then: the panel is shown with its button disabled and the "BUS OFFLINE" body; a press publishes
    nothing
- verify-machine: widget unit tests for the two configurations, the `bus_offline` disabled rendering,
  the on-change-only publish, and the hint's expiry under an injected clock ([DD-ICL-LAUNCH-HINT] is
  RESOLVED, so this leg is unconditional). A test asserts the panel module contains NO dedupe, NO timer
  that gates publishing, and NO spawn — and specifically that `LAUNCH_HINT_S` is read ONLY where the
  sub-line's text is chosen.
- verify-human: David sees the launch card where the gauge was, presses it, and — after answering the
  dialogs — watches the gauge come back in the SAME console window ([CXC-ICL-ACCEPT] ICL-C11).
- realizes: [DX-LEAF-OWNS-BEHAVIOR]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

---

## 4. The intent — control is a peer, including for instantiation

### [CXC-ICL-INTENT] `_app/intelligence/open` — a new row on the EXISTING `_app/*/open` plane

- definition: the launch intent is `_app/intelligence/open`, published at GLOBAL scope
  (`composeio/global/_app/intelligence/open`), carrying an optional payload
  `{"id": "<intelligence-id>"}`.
- requirement: **(it must fit the EXISTING subscription — do not widen it)** `app_launcher`
  subscribes
  `composeio/global/_app/+/open` and extracts `<target>` from the last three topic segments. Naming the
  intent `_app/intelligence/open` means it is received with NO subscription change, NO new topic
  pattern, and NO second daemon. An intent named `_app/harness/launch` would NOT be received at all —
  the wildcard requires `open` as the final segment. This is why the key is `intelligence` and the verb
  is `open` ([DD-ICL-INTENT-KEY]).
- requirement: **(payload plumbing — the one real signature change)** `AppLauncher.open()` today
  takes only
  `target` and the payload is discarded after `parse_intent`. It MUST become
  `open(target: str, payload: Any = None) -> str`, with `payload` threaded from `on_message`. Every
  existing call site passes nothing and behaves identically. Without this the launch cannot be
  per-`<id>` and the multi-instance future ([CXC-IH-5A]) is closed off at birth.
- requirement: **(payload parsing is DEFENSIVE, and the id is VALIDATED)** the id is read from the
  bus
  envelope (`{val: {...}}`) or the bare dict; it MUST match `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}$` and MUST
  otherwise be REFUSED with a log line, never substituted. It becomes a path segment (`INSTANCE_HOME`)
  and a shell word ([CXC-ICL-TERMINAL]); an unvalidated id is a command-injection vector.
- default: absent or empty payload id => `architect`. Stated, not inferred.
- requirement: **(`parse_intent` already tolerates this payload — do not change it)** `parse_intent`
  reads
  `d.get("open", d.get("path", True))`, so `{"id": "architect"}` evaluates truthy and opens. A falsy
  token (`""`, `off`, `none`, `false`, `0`, `close`) is a no-op, as for every other `_app/*` intent.
  This spec MUST NOT alter `parse_intent`; it is shared with the doc/decisions launchers.
- scenario:
  - when: a human presses the launch button, OR an Intelligence publishes
    `_app/intelligence/open = {"id": "architect"}`
  - then: the identical intent is on the bus and `app_launcher` takes the identical path — the two
    triggers are indistinguishable downstream (this IS acceptance criterion ICL-C5)
- uses: [CXC-LB-INTENT-BINDING], [CXC-LB-INTENT-TABLE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [CXC-ICL-SPAWN-ONCE] Spawn-once reuses `spawned_windows` SKIP — but the handle must model the LAUNCH, not `osascript`

- requirement: the anti-double-spawn guarantee is `spawned_windows.SpawnedWindows(SKIP)`, keyed on the
  INTELLIGENCE ID, reused by reference. `app_launcher` already holds exactly that tracker; the
  `intelligence` target uses `key = f"intelligence:{id}"`. SKIP's documented semantics are precisely
  what is wanted: "a launch whose key is already live YIELDS (return the incumbent, spawn nothing)".
  No new lock, no new module, no new policy.
- known-hazard: **(LOAD-BEARING — the naive reuse silently does nothing)** `SpawnedWindows` decides
  liveness with `_alive(proc)` = `proc.poll() is None`. For a terminal target the spawned process is
  `osascript`, which returns in MILLISECONDS after telling Terminal to run the script. Register that
  handle and the key is reaped almost immediately, the second press spawns a second Terminal, and the
  guard reads as present while doing nothing. This is the single most likely way this slice ships
  broken.
- requirement: **(the fix is a HANDLE, and the tracker was built for it)** `spawned_windows` is
  deliberately duck-typed — "a process handle is anything with `.poll()` (alive iff it returns None)
  ... this keeps the tracker testable with a fake proc and unentangled from paho/skia". The
  `intelligence` target therefore registers a `LaunchHandle`, not the `osascript` `Popen`:
  - `LaunchHandle.poll()` returns `None` (ALIVE) while the launch is still in flight, and `0` once it
    is not, so `SpawnedWindows.reap()` clears the slot on its own with no extra bookkeeping.
  - In flight means: the id's `channel/alive` has NOT yet been seen since this launch, AND fewer than
    `LAUNCH_SETTLE_S` (**default 90.0 s**) have elapsed since the spawn.
  - It keeps the `osascript` `Popen` as `.inner` for logging/diagnostics only.
  Both terminating conditions are required: the alive-arrival is the SUCCESS exit (the guard hands off
  to [CXC-ICL-PEER-GUARD]), and the timeout is the FAILURE exit (a launch the human abandoned must not
  block a retry forever).
- rationale: **(for 90 s)** the human must answer the startup dialogs before `channel.mjs` starts
  beating,
  so this is a human-latency interval, not a machine one. Too short reopens the double-spawn; too long
  blocks a legitimate retry. It is a module constant, not a magic number at the call site.
- consequence: **(this is what lets [CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY] have no third state)** because the dedupe
  is in
  the daemon, the button may stay on screen and pressable for the whole launch window. A double-click
  and a double-publish receive the identical `skipped` outcome, which is what keeps the human path and
  the bus path symmetric.
- scenario:
  - when: the intent is published twice, 1 s apart, and no `channel/alive` has arrived
  - then: exactly one Terminal is spawned and the second call returns the tracker's `skipped` action
- scenario:
  - when: the human abandons the launch and `LAUNCH_SETTLE_S` elapses with no `channel/alive`
  - then: the `LaunchHandle` reads not-alive, the tracker reaps the slot, and the next press launches
    fresh
- verify-machine: (a) two `open("intelligence")` calls 1 s apart under an injected clock => exactly ONE
  spawn, the second returning the `skipped` action; (b) a third call after `LAUNCH_SETTLE_S` => a fresh
  spawn; (c) a call after the id's `channel/alive` arrives => the slot is reaped and the PEER GUARD
  (not the tracker) is what refuses; (d) a REGRESSION test that registering the raw `osascript` handle
  instead of the `LaunchHandle` FAILS leg (a) — so the naive reuse cannot come back unnoticed.
- uses: [DX-NARROW-WAIST]
- relates-to: [CXC-ICL-PEER-GUARD]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [CXC-ICL-PEER-GUARD] Never launch beside a LIVE HARNESS — and the guard keys on HARNESSED, not on reachable

- requirement: **(the predicate, corrected)** `app_launcher` MUST refuse the `intelligence` target
  when
  that id is already **HARNESSED** — `self/daemons[intelligence_launcher].status == "up"` and fresh
  ([CXC-ICL-PRESENCE]). It MUST NOT refuse merely because `channel/alive` is fresh.
- rationale: **(why reachability is the WRONG predicate here)** the `unharnessed` state
  ([CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY] state 2) is a fresh `channel/alive` with NO launcher. That is precisely the
  case where a launch is LEGITIMATE and needed. A guard keyed on reachability would refuse it — the
  same inversion, one layer down, that [CXC-ICL-PRESENCE] corrects at the pane. The two guards read the
  same two signals and must key on the same halves of them.
- known-hazard: **(what the guard protects)** two launchers relaying `intelligence/<id>/out` would
  DOUBLE-INJECT every actuation ([CXC-IC-SINGLETON]). Under [CXC-ICL-SINGLETON-YIELD] a second launcher
  now YIELDS rather than reaping the first, so the failure mode is no longer "a stray intent kills a
  working harness" — it is "a Terminal opens, the launcher yields loudly, and nothing happens." That is
  a much better failure, but it is still a wasted window and a confusing one, so the guard refuses
  BEFORE spawning rather than relying on the yield.
- requirement: **(how the launcher knows)** `app_launcher` subscribes
  `composeio/global/self/daemons` and
  stamps arrival time (`time.monotonic()`), treating the map as authoritative while
  `now - last_arrival <= DAEMONS_FRESH_S` (**default 15.0 s**, the SAME constant the panel uses —
  defined once and asserted equal by a test, never typed twice). It ALSO subscribes
  `composeio/global/intelligence/+/channel/alive` for the [CXC-ICL-SPAWN-ONCE] handle's success-exit,
  which is a different use of a different signal.
- default: **(UNKNOWN harness state ALLOWS the launch)** with `self/daemons` stale or absent, the
  launcher
  PROCEEDS. The pane will not be offering the button in that case anyway ([CXC-ICL-PRESENCE] renders
  the gauge under `harness_unknown`), so an intent arriving there came from an Intelligence that asked
  deliberately, and [CXC-ICL-SINGLETON-YIELD] makes the downside a loud no-op rather than a reap.
  Refusing on unknown would make a `daemon_watch` outage silently disable launching entirely.
- requirement: on `open("intelligence", ...)` with the id HARNESSED, the launcher logs and returns
  `already harnessed (refused): <id>` and spawns NOTHING. It MUST NOT reap, kill, or supersede
  anything — this spec never terminates a running Intelligence or a running launcher.
- known-hazard: **(the decide-then-spawn race, stated because it WILL be hit)** the harness check
  and the
  spawn are not atomic. A launcher can come up in the window between the check and the Terminal
  actually starting it. The narrowing is [CXC-ICL-SPAWN-ONCE] (at most one spawn per settle window)
  plus the flock itself (at most one LAUNCHER — and under [CXC-ICL-SINGLETON-YIELD] deterministically
  the INCUMBENT, with the loser exiting loudly). The race cannot produce two relaying launchers, only,
  at worst, one wasted Terminal carrying a visible explanation. That residual is ACCEPTED and named.
- scenario:
  - when: `self/daemons` reports `intelligence_launcher: up` and the intent is published
  - then: nothing spawns, nothing is reaped, and the return reads `already harnessed (refused)`
- scenario:
  - when: `channel/alive` is fresh but `intelligence_launcher` is `down` (the unharnessed case)
  - then: the launch PROCEEDS — reachability alone never refuses
- verify-machine: publish `self/daemons` with `intelligence_launcher: up`, drive
  `open("intelligence", {"id": "architect"})` under an injected clock, assert ZERO spawns and the
  `already harnessed (refused)` return; then publish `status: down` WITH a fresh `channel/alive` and
  assert the spawn HAPPENS (the regression guard for the corrected predicate); then let `self/daemons`
  go stale and assert the spawn still happens.
- upholds: [CXC-IC-SINGLETON]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [CXC-ICL-SINGLETON-YIELD] The launcher's singleton becomes YIELD-IF-HELD + LOUD — a re-ratification of [CXC-IC-SINGLETON]

- decision: `launcher.py` acquires `intelligence-launcher-<id>` with **yield-if-held**
  (`acquire_singleton`), not **supersede** (`acquire_singleton_superseding`). On a held lock it does
  NOT spawn `claude`; it reports LOUDLY and exits.
- premise: **(David, 2026-08-10)** "My design intent is to remove myself from having to call the
  transport.sh from the CLI, and for that to not even be a supported invocation path. Intent is launch
  the IC, auto-connect to a running ComposeIO-Architect, and if none running, launch one from within
  the IC."
- why-supersede-existed: **(and why the reason expires)** the code's own comment states it —
  "A plain yield-if-held flock would make a RESUME start exit and SILENTLY fail the restart, so we
  SUPERSEDE." That is a workaround for an UNCOORDINATED world with multiple entry points, where a
  lingering predecessor could block a legitimate relaunch and the human would never learn why. With
  the IC as the SOLE entry point ([CXC-ICL-CLI-UNSUPPORTED]) that race cannot arise: the console
  refuses to launch beside a live harness ([CXC-ICL-PEER-GUARD]) instead of racing a flock, so a held
  lock stops being a routine condition to bulldoze and becomes an ANOMALY worth reporting.
- requirement: **(LOUD, not silent — the original defect must not return)** on a held lock the
  launcher
  MUST print, to the Terminal the human is looking at, the holder's pid, the lock name, and the
  instruction to check the existing session — then exit non-zero, which under [CXC-ICL-TERMINAL] leaves
  the window OPEN with the message visible. The silent-restart-failure that supersede was built to
  prevent is cured by VISIBILITY, not by bulldozing: the human now finds out immediately, in the window
  they just opened, which is the one place they are certainly looking.
- rejected-alternative: **("the console owns the lifecycle explicitly and never needs supersede")**
  it
  CANNOT, and the reason is structural, not preferential. `app_launcher` spawns
  `osascript -e 'tell application "Terminal" to do script ...'`; `osascript` returns in milliseconds
  and the resulting shell — and the `launcher.py` beneath it — are children of **Terminal.app**, not of
  `app_launcher`. The console therefore never holds a handle on the launcher process and structurally
  cannot reap it. (This is the same fact that makes [CXC-ICL-SPAWN-ONCE] need a `LaunchHandle`.) With
  no ownership possible through this seam, the flock is the ONLY coordination point, so it must be the
  one that behaves correctly.
- requirement: **(unconditional, no env flag)** the flip applies to EVERY invocation, not only
  console-initiated ones. A conditional supersede would mean the dangerous behaviour survives on
  exactly the path being deprecated. It is also strictly safer for a human running the script by hand
  today: the current behaviour reaps a live, working harness; the new behaviour refuses and says so.
- spec-drift-to-reconcile: **(flagged, cross-referenced so it is recorded ONCE)**
  [CXC-TERM-SINGLETON] in
  [SPEC:CXC-TERM] requires the terminal widget to acquire the lock via `acquire_singleton_superseding`
  and states "this spec MUST NOT change `acquire_singleton_superseding`'s semantics... If superseding
  turns out to be wrong for a windowed host, that is a re-ratification of [CXC-IC-SINGLETON], not a
  local exception." THIS NODE IS THAT RE-RATIFICATION. [CXC-TERM-SINGLETON] MUST be updated to
  yield-if-held + the degraded banner when this lands, and its hazard framing ("a human running
  `transport.sh` would supersede and reap the console's child") is resolved from the other direction:
  under [CXC-ICL-CLI-UNSUPPORTED] that human path ceases to exist. Whichever spec builds first carries
  the change; the second reconciles.
- scenario:
  - when: a launcher is started for an id whose lock is already held
  - then: no `claude` is spawned, the holder's pid is named in the Terminal, and the process exits
    non-zero with the window still open
- scenario:
  - when: the previous launcher crashed
  - then: the flock released on death, the lock is free, and the launch proceeds normally — a crash
    never blocks a restart, which is the one property supersede genuinely bought and which yield
    retains
- verify-machine: a test acquires the lock out-of-process, runs the launcher, and asserts (a) NO child
  spawn, (b) a non-zero exit, (c) the holder pid appears in stderr; plus a grep guard that
  `acquire_singleton_superseding` has no remaining caller in `launcher.py`.
- verify-human: David confirms the message is legible in the Terminal window when he triggers it
  deliberately — a loud failure nobody can read is a silent one.
- re-ratifies: [CXC-IC-SINGLETON] (the launcher case only; the flock NAME, the one-launcher-per-id
  invariant, and the double-injection rationale are all unchanged — only the collision POLICY moves)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [CXC-ICL-CLI-UNSUPPORTED] CLI `transport.sh` invocation becomes UNSUPPORTED — the IC is the sole entry point

- decision: **(David, 2026-08-10)** calling `transport.sh` from the CLI is not merely discouraged,
  it
  ceases to be a SUPPORTED invocation path. The supported flow is: launch the IC; it auto-connects to a
  running harnessed Architect; if none is running, it launches one.
- consequence: **(for `transport.sh`)** it SURVIVES as an internal implementation detail that
  `launch-instance.sh` composes with ([CXC-ICL-ENTRY]), not as a user-facing entry point. Its
  human-facing documentation — the usage banner, the "run this from the SAME directory" instruction,
  and the `README.md` "transport.sh" section — MUST be re-framed as internal, so a future reader does
  not restore the CLI ritual from the docs after the code stopped supporting it.
- constraint: **(what "unsupported" does and does not mean)** the script is not deleted and is not
  booby-trapped. Running it by hand still works and is the debugging path; what changes is that it
  carries no compatibility promise, and that the singleton behaviour it inherits is now
  yield-if-held + loud ([CXC-ICL-SINGLETON-YIELD]) rather than reap-the-incumbent.
- dependency: **(flagged)** if the terminal widget ([SPEC:CXC-TERM]) lands, the IC hosts the PTY
  itself and
  invokes `launcher.py` directly — at which point `transport.sh` has no remaining caller and may retire
  ENTIRELY. This spec does not retire it, because that decision belongs with whichever slice makes the
  last caller disappear. Recorded here so the retirement is a planned step and not an orphan discovered
  later.
- rationale: the whole coordination story in this spec — one guard, one flock owner, one lifecycle —
  rests on there being ONE entry point. Every multi-entry-point defense this corpus has written
  (supersede, the double-injection guard, [CXC-TERM-SINGLETON]'s degraded banner) is a cost paid for a
  second door. Closing the door is cheaper than defending it.
- relates-to: [CXC-ICL-SINGLETON-YIELD], [CXC-ICL-ENTRY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

---

## 5. The spawn — a Terminal, a cwd, and a bootstrapped cold start

### [CXC-ICL-TERMINAL] Extend the EXISTING `TERMINAL` kind — but it must use `osascript`, because `open -a` passes no arguments

- ratified: David, 2026-08-10 — "Agree that the button has to spawn a terminal running transport.sh —
  reuse what's present." `app_launcher` is extended; no new spawner is written.
- what-is-already-present: `app_launcher` DOES carry a `TERMINAL` spawn kind, and `_terminal_argv`
  returns `["open", "-a", "Terminal", <script>]` on darwin and `None` elsewhere. NO table row uses it
  today, and its own docstring flags it: "this opens the launcher with no per-Intelligence id/env — the
  exact harness invocation is unresolved and awaits David's ratification." This node is that
  resolution.
- known-hazard: **(why `open -a` alone cannot serve it)** `open -a Terminal <file>` runs the file
  with **NO
  ARGUMENTS** and in the **USER'S LOGIN ENVIRONMENT**, not the launcher's. There is therefore no way to
  pass the intelligence id — so `open -a` can only ever launch the DEFAULT instance, and the
  control-is-a-peer per-id payload ([CXC-ICL-INTENT]) would be silently dropped. (`transport.sh` is
  worse still under `open -a`: its `SESSION="${1:?usage...}"` exits immediately with a usage message,
  producing a Terminal that flashes and dies.)
- requirement: `_terminal_argv` is CHANGED to
  `["osascript", "-e", 'tell application "Terminal" to do script "<CMD>"', "-e", 'tell application "Terminal" to activate']`,
  where `<CMD>` is a fully-formed command line built by the launcher. `do script` runs an arbitrary
  command WITH arguments in a new Terminal window; `activate` raises it. Raising is REQUIRED, not
  cosmetic: a Terminal spawned behind the console with a dialog waiting is indistinguishable from a
  launch that hung.
- requirement: **(`<CMD>` construction is CLOSED, never templated from a payload)** `<CMD>` is
  composed
  ONLY from (a) the repo-scoped absolute path of the entry script ([CXC-ICL-ENTRY], resolved through
  the existing `resolve_scene` repo guard) and (b) the VALIDATED id ([CXC-ICL-INTENT]). Each component
  is `shlex.quote`d before interpolation and the result is escaped for AppleScript (`\` and `"`). No
  payload string reaches `<CMD>` unvalidated.
- requirement: **(the window MUST NOT close on failure)** the entry script MUST NOT end in a form
  that
  closes the window on a non-zero exit. On failure it prints the failing command and its exit code and
  drops to an interactive shell (`exec "$SHELL" -i` after the error message). A Terminal that opens and
  closes is a launch failure with no evidence — the silent-failure class again.
- requirement: **(non-darwin)** `_terminal_argv` returns `None` on any non-darwin platform today. It
  MUST
  continue to, and `open("intelligence")` MUST then return an explicit
  `no terminal-open on this platform (<plat>) — run intelligence/launcher/launch-instance.sh manually`.
  A silent no-op is forbidden. The console is an explicitly macOS/desktop-tier surface
  ([CXC-LB-TIER-CONSOLE]); this is that scope, stated.
- requirement: **(the table row)** `TARGET_TABLE` gains exactly one row —
  `"intelligence": Target("intelligence", TERMINAL, command="intelligence/launcher/launch-instance.sh", note="...")`.
  `registered_targets()` grows by one and the register-guard selftest's roster set is updated in the
  same commit ([CXC-LB-INTENT-TABLE]).
- scenario:
  - when: `open("intelligence", {"id": "architect"})` is routed on a darwin host
  - then: the built argv is an `osascript` `do script` + `activate` pair whose command names the
    repo-resolved `launch-instance.sh` and the id `architect`
- scenario:
  - when: the same intent is routed on a non-darwin host
  - then: nothing spawns and the return names the manual command to run
- verify-machine: a selftest check asserts the built argv is an `osascript` invocation containing the
  repo-resolved script path and the validated id; and a negative check that an id of `a; rm -rf /` is
  REFUSED by the id regex and never reaches `<CMD>`.
- uses: [CXC-LB-LAUNCHER]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [CXC-ICL-CWD] The launch cwd is the INSTANCE HOME — a bus intent has no "current directory"

- constraint: `transport.sh` deliberately does NOT `cd`; it runs from whatever cwd invoked it. A launch
  from the Console has no invoking cwd to inherit — a bus intent arrives in a daemon whose cwd is the
  repo root, which is emphatically NOT where an instance should live. The cwd must therefore be picked
  EXPLICITLY. (This was open design question B; it stands, and this is its answer.)
- requirement: the launch cwd is `INSTANCE_HOME`, default `~/compose-instances/<id>/`, created if
  absent. This is [DEC-INSTANCE-KEYED-HOMES] / [CXC-IH-HOMES] applied: one instance, one home; the key
  is the INSTANCE's role/identity, not the project — because an instance works across many Compose*
  projects and keying by project re-fragments it the moment it moves between them. `transport.sh`
  already implements exactly this (`INSTANCE_HOME` -> `mkdir -p` + `cd`), so the console-initiated path
  SETS THE VARIABLE and reuses that code rather than inventing a cd of its own.
- consequence: **(the cold-start choice makes this clean)** because every console-initiated launch
  is a
  COLD start ([CXC-ICL-COLD]), the `--resume` re-pinning caveat that complicates instance homes
  ("`--resume` re-pins to the project dir the session was CREATED in, so this re-keys only a session
  cold-started in the instance home") does not apply at all. Every session this path creates is created
  IN the instance home, which is exactly the condition that decision wanted and could not previously
  guarantee. The console launch is what finally makes instance-keyed homes the default.
- requirement: **(reach the repos from the home)** the Compose* projects are reached from the
  instance home
  as `additionalDirectories` in `$INSTANCE_HOME/.claude/settings*`, per [CXC-IH-HOMES]. The entry
  script MUST NOT `cd` into a repo to "help"; that would collapse the instance back onto a project key.
- default: `INSTANCE_HOME` may be overridden per launch by the environment
  `COMPOSEIO_INSTANCE_HOME_<ID>`; unset means the stated default. Nothing is inferred from the
  console's own cwd.
- uses: [CXC-IH-HOMES]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [CXC-ICL-COLD] Always COLD START, always WITH the re-register bootstrap — and correct the launcher prose that calls it experimental

- decision: (settled here; this was open design question A, and the correction below dissolves it)
  a console-initiated launch is ALWAYS a cold start, run WITH the launcher's re-register bootstrap. It
  does NOT search for, offer, or resume a prior session.
- correction: (David, 2026-08-10) "The re-register bootstrap is not experimental any longer, it works
  quite well — it should be the default for new instances." The premise that made a naive Launch button
  produce an UNORIENTED instance is therefore false. A cold start with the bootstrap is a legitimate,
  oriented instance, and the entire reason to prefer resume evaporates.
- requirement: (AMENDED 2026-08-11 — the delivery MECHANISM changed; the rule did not) the entry
  script sets `BOOTSTRAP=1` for every console-initiated launch. The bootstrap prompt is built
  from the meta-object manifest by `build_bootstrap_prompt`, which points at the
  `role_descriptor` / `soul` / `dusk_gifts` artifacts BY PATH and never inlines their contents —
  that referencing discipline is preserved unchanged. What changed is HOW the prompt reaches the
  child: it is passed as the child's POSITIONAL PROMPT ARGUMENT at exec time
  ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV]), not typed into the PTY after a delay. There is NO
  `BOOTSTRAP_DELAY` — the knob, the timer and the whole startup-injection path are DELETED, not
  defaulted ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-SETTLE-RETIRED]). This node's INTENT is untouched: a
  console-initiated launch is still always cold and still always bootstrapped.
- requirement: (manifest resolution is a CHAIN, added 2026-08-11) the manifest is resolved
  `META_MANIFEST` -> `intelligence/meta/<id>.meta.json` -> `intelligence/meta/<role>.meta.json`
  -> REFUSE LOUDLY ([CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN]). The single-path resolution this node was written
  against cannot serve N instances of one role, and a launch that cannot orient must refuse
  visibly rather than log and continue. `BOOTSTRAP=0` remains the explicit opt-out and is the
  only way to get an unoriented instance on purpose.
- requirement: (IN SCOPE for this build — fix the stale prose, do not leave the contradiction)
  `intelligence/launcher/launcher.py` currently defaults `BOOTSTRAP` OFF and describes it as
  "optional, experimental ... best-effort ... may collide with the human still answering dialogs" in
  BOTH its module docstring and its config table, and `intelligence/launcher/README.md` repeats it
  ("Cold start & the optional bootstrap (default OFF)" and the cold-start column of its mode table).
  That prose is now OUT OF DATE. This build MUST (a) flip the default to ON, (b) correct all three
  prose sites, and (c) keep `BOOTSTRAP=0` as the explicit opt-OUT. Shipping a spec that says "default
  on" against code and docs that say "experimental, default off" leaves the next reader to discover
  the contradiction and guess which is true — which is the drift class this corpus exists to kill.
- rationale: (why cold and not resume, now that cold is oriented) one button, one predictable
  behaviour. The resume path would have required deriving Claude Code's project-dir key by MIRRORING a
  third-party tool's path encoding, then guessing which `.jsonl` the human meant ("the newest one whose
  mtime is old enough that the session is probably not still running") — a heuristic stacked on an
  remains the explicit human act for re-hosting a SPECIFIC session, and it is unchanged. The machine
  does the thing it can do correctly every time; the human keeps the thing that needs judgement.
- constraint: because the launch is always cold, the entry script takes ONE optional argument (the id)
- scenario:
  - when: the console launches an instance and the human answers the startup dialogs
  - then: the bootstrap prompt was already on the child's argv at exec time, NOTHING is typed
    into the PTY at any point, and the instance comes up ORIENTED off its referenced gifts —
    with no prior session resumed
- scenario:
  - when: an operator sets `BOOTSTRAP=0` before running the entry script by hand
  - then: no prompt argument is passed and nothing is injected, exactly as the pre-change default
    behaved
- scenario:
  - when: neither `<id>.meta.json` nor `<role>.meta.json` exists and `BOOTSTRAP` is not `0`
  - then: the launch REFUSES, naming every path it tried — it does not start an unoriented
    instance and log about it ([CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN])
- verify-machine: a test asserts the entry script exports `BOOTSTRAP=1` and passes NO `RESUME_SESSION`;
  and a grep guard that no "experimental" characterization of the bootstrap survives in
  `launcher.py` / `README.md` after the prose fix.
- build-status: (2026-08-11 — the code half is DONE; the entry script is NOT) (a), (b) and (c) all landed —
  the default is ON (`ptycore.bootstrap_enabled`, unset/blank means ON), all three prose sites are
  corrected, and `BOOTSTRAP=0` is the explicit opt-OUT. The grep guard exists and ALLOWS the line that
  QUOTES David's correction ("is not experimental any longer"); quoting the correction is the opposite
  of repeating the claim. TWO silent no-ops were found while doing it and are also fixed: `main()`
  gated the bootstrap on `META_MANIFEST` being SET (it is unset by default, so even `BOOTSTRAP=1`
  injected NOTHING — the manifest now defaults to `intelligence/meta/<id>.meta.json`), and the
  injection was `inject(prompt + "\n")`, a bulk write ending in a LINE FEED — the TUI's newline, not
  its submit — so it would have typed the whole prompt and then waited forever for a human Return. It
  now goes through the three-phase `inject_prompt` (clear -> bracketed body -> `\r` submit).
  STILL OPEN: `intelligence/launcher/launch-instance.sh` ([CXC-ICL-ENTRY]) does not exist, so the
  entry-script leg of this verify-machine cannot pass yet.
- consequence: (the widget host, added 2026-08-11, CORRECTED the same day) the terminal widget
  ([SPEC:CXC-TERM]) implements this node too — a cold spawn in the widget carries the same
  bootstrap, off the same builder, with the same `BOOTSTRAP=0` opt-out. The settle delay both
  hosts shared is GONE from both: on its first live firing it landed in a blocking `Enter y/n:`
  dialog read and echoed `^[[200~` as literal text, which is what retired the mechanism
  ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-SETTLE-RETIRED]). [CXC-TERM-SUPERSEDES-55] recorded this node as SURVIVING
  UNCHANGED into that host, and it still does — the RULE survived; only its delivery changed.
- known-gap: (2026-08-11, this node's verify-machine now names a knob that will not exist) the
  `BOOTSTRAP_DELAY` grep guard and the "leaves BOOTSTRAP_DELAY at 3.0" assertion are replaced by
  their inverse — a guard that `BOOTSTRAP_DELAY` appears NOWHERE. The entry script
  ([CXC-ICL-ENTRY]) still does not exist, so this leg of the verify remains unrunnable either
  way; it is named here rather than quietly re-scoped.
- verify-human: David confirms on the glass that a console-launched instance comes up ORIENTED — it
  re-registers off its gifts without being told to ([CXC-ICL-ACCEPT] ICL-C11).
- change: "2026-08-11 (third pass): bootstrap DELIVERY moves from a timed PTY injection to the child's positional prompt argument, and manifest resolution becomes a chain that refuses loudly. The RULE this node states — always cold, always bootstrapped, `BOOTSTRAP=0` as the opt-out — is unchanged."
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "This node required the entry script to set `BOOTSTRAP=1` and leave `BOOTSTRAP_DELAY` at its 3.0 s default, and its scenario read 'the launcher types the re-register bootstrap after BOOTSTRAP_DELAY'. Manifest resolution was a single path, `intelligence/meta/<id>.meta.json`."
    reasoning: "The first live spawn of a real `claude` fired the bootstrap into a blocking dialog read. The delay is a fixed timer standing in for a state check against a startup with an unbounded number of human-gated reads, so no value of it is correct; and `claude [options] [command] [prompt]` means the typing was never necessary. The manifest chain follows from the same spawn: a hosted child now carries a DISTINCT id ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]), so a single `<id>.meta.json` leg misses for every child that is not hand-curated."
    rejected_alternative: "Amend only [SPEC:CXC-TERM] and leave this node's requirement and scenario naming `BOOTSTRAP_DELAY` — rejected outright: two ratified specs disagreeing about the same mechanism is exactly the contradiction this node itself had to correct once already, when the code called the bootstrap 'experimental, default off' against a spec that said 'default on'. The next reader would have to guess which is true."
    invalidating_assumption: "That the positional prompt reaches the session in interactive mode regardless of startup gates. Unmeasured; the as-deployed census profile ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS-CONFIG-FIDELITY]) is what measures it, and TERM-C13 is the glass gate. A refutation does NOT restore the settle delay — typing at startup stays banned ([CXC-ICL-CONSTRAINT-PTY])."
- relates-to: [DD-ICL-COLD-VS-RESUME], [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV], [CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [CXC-ICL-ENTRY] ONE entry script — `launch-instance.sh` composes with `transport.sh`, it does not fork it

- definition: a new script `intelligence/launcher/launch-instance.sh`, the single entry point the
  Terminal runs. Signature: `launch-instance.sh [<id>]`, id defaulting to `architect`. There is no
  session argument ([CXC-ICL-COLD]).
- requirement: **(compose, never fork)** the script sets `INSTANCE_HOME` ([CXC-ICL-CWD]),
  `INTELLIGENCE_ID`, `COMPOSEIO_REPO_ROOT`, and `BOOTSTRAP=1`, guarantees the three-server `.mcp.json`
  in the instance home, and `exec`s `launcher.py` with `RESUME_SESSION` UNSET. The `.mcp.json` writer
  MUST be SHARED with `transport.sh` (a sourced helper, or a `transport.sh --ensure-mcp-only` mode),
  NOT copy-pasted. A second copy of that manifest is the exact fork-divergence this corpus fails builds
  over ([DX-NARROW-WAIST]) — and it is the file that decides whether the instance has a channel at all,
  so a drifted copy means an instance that comes up mute and a console that shows a launch button
  forever.
- requirement: **(it is the ONLY thing the table row names)** `TARGET_TABLE`'s `command` is this
  script and
  nothing else. The repo-scoped `resolve_scene` guard runs on it as on every other target path.
- requirement: **(usable by hand, identically)** a human running
  `intelligence/launcher/launch-instance.sh architect` in a terminal MUST get the identical result to a
  button press. The console path is a CALLER of the script, not a privileged path around it —
  otherwise the button and the manual ritual drift, and the ritual is what gets debugged at 2am.
- requirement: **(loud preamble)** before exec'ing, the script echoes the resolved plan — id,
  instance
  home, cold+bootstrap, claude binary, broker — so the Terminal window itself is the audit record of
  what was launched. The launcher already logs every keystroke it injects; the launch decision deserves
  the same treatment.
- verify-machine: a shell-level test asserts `launch-instance.sh --dry-run architect` prints the plan
  and exec's nothing, that it exports `BOOTSTRAP=1` and no `RESUME_SESSION`, and a grep guard that the
  three-server `.mcp.json` JSON literal appears exactly ONCE in the tree.
- uses: [DX-NARROW-WAIST]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

---

## 6. Errors and boundaries — every one the design implies, written down

### [CXC-ICL-BOUNDARIES] The failure matrix — what happens, and what is VISIBLE, in each

- definition: the enumeration. A row's "surfaced as" column is normative: every failure is visible
  somewhere a human or a test can see it. There is no row whose behaviour is "nothing happens".

| # | Condition | Behaviour | Surfaced as |
|---|-----------|-----------|-------------|
| 1 | Intent with no payload | id defaults to `architect` | normal launch |
| 2 | Intent with an id failing `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}$` | REFUSED, no spawn | log `refused (bad id)` |
| 3 | Intent with a falsy payload (`""`, `off`, `false`, `0`, `close`) | no-op, as every `_app/*` intent | nothing (correct — a close verb has no meaning here) |
| 4 | Target id already HARNESSED ([CXC-ICL-PEER-GUARD]) | REFUSED, nothing reaped | log `already harnessed (refused)` |
| 4b | Target id REACHABLE but UNHARNESSED | launch PROCEEDS — this is the case the feature exists for | the `unharnessed` warning pane preceded it |
| 4c | `self/daemons` stale/absent at intent time | launch PROCEEDS ([CXC-ICL-PEER-GUARD] default) | debug log naming the stale harness read |
| 4d | The flock is held when the launcher starts | NO child spawned; loud message + non-zero exit; window stays open ([CXC-ICL-SINGLETON-YIELD]) | the Terminal window itself, naming the holder pid |
| 5 | A launch for this id already in flight ([CXC-ICL-SPAWN-ONCE]) | SKIPPED by the tracker | log `already open (skip)`; the button stays on screen and pressable |
| 6 | `launch-instance.sh` missing or outside the repo | REFUSED by `resolve_scene` | log `refused (command missing/out-of-repo)` |
| 7 | Non-darwin platform | REFUSED with the manual command named | log |
| 8 | `osascript` non-zero / Terminal not installed | the `LaunchHandle` reads NOT-alive immediately, so the slot clears and a retry is possible at once | log with the captured stderr |
| 9 | Human never answers the dialogs | the `LaunchHandle` expires at `LAUNCH_SETTLE_S` and the slot clears, so pressing again launches fresh | the button was never hidden; a re-press just works |
| 10 | `claude` fails inside the Terminal | window STAYS OPEN with the error and an interactive shell | the Terminal window itself |
| 11 | Bus down at press time | button DISABLED with "BUS OFFLINE" ([CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY]) | the panel body |
| 12 | `console/link/state` never published (a scene with no `link_health`) | pane stays on the gauge — today's behaviour exactly | nothing; the additive-superset property ([CXC-ICL-PANE-SWAP]) |
| 13 | The instance comes up but its `.mcp.json` lacks the channel server | no `channel/alive` ever arrives; harness reads `up`, so the pane shows `orphaned` with the button DISABLED and points at the Terminal | the `orphaned` body — which is exactly right: a launcher IS running |
| 14 | `daemon_watch` itself is down | harness UNKNOWN; the pane shows the gauge plus `harness: unknown` — never a false alarm, never a silent guess ([CXC-ICL-PRESENCE]) | the marker |
| 15 | Two instances briefly share an id after an `unharnessed` launch | CANNOT HAPPEN — a state-2 launch goes out under the DISTINCT recovery id `recovery-architect-2` ([DD-ICL-UNHARNESSED-ACTION]), so `intelligence/<id>/*` and `self/<id>/*` keep exactly one publisher each and [CXC-IH-5A]'s clobber never arises | CLOSED BY CONSTRUCTION, not a mitigated hazard — the panel body names the recovery id it will launch and instructs the human to exit NOTHING; the mechanical guard is a test asserting the state-2 intent carries `recovery-architect-2` and never the canonical id, added to the truth-table test in [CXC-ICL-PRESENCE] |
| 16 | Channel REACHABLE but DEGRADED (`delivery != "ok"`, or `unacked` climbing across `DEGRADED_CLIMB_N` payloads) | the PRESENCE states are UNCHANGED — the gauge stays, the launch button stays hidden, nothing is launched; the condition is SURFACED, never acted on ([CXC-ICL-PRESENCE], [CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY]) | the header dot goes amber and `link_label` + `composer_send_label` read `DEGRADED`; the `intelligence_card` shows `channel: degraded — messages may not be arriving` |
| 17 | The verdict is `degraded` but a `map:` bound to `console/link/state` has no `degraded` key | that surface silently renders its `default:` — the HEALTHY look — which is a green ENGAGED header over a channel that is not delivering | CANNOT SHIP: [CXC-ICL-ACCEPT] ICL-C19 leg (b) enumerates every such binding and fails the build if one lacks the key |
- constraint: rows 8, 9 and 10 are the three places a launch can fail AFTER the intent was accepted.
  All three MUST leave the system immediately re-launchable — 8 and 9 by the `LaunchHandle` clearing
  its slot, 10 by the window staying open. A failure that leaves the button permanently inert is worse
  than the original problem.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

---

## 7. Acceptance — machine signal AND, for every human-perceptible surface, a glass gate

### [CXC-ICL-ACCEPT] The acceptance criteria

- definition: two-tier by construction — a machine signal is necessary and, for any surface a human
  perceives, never sufficient. ICL-C1..C6 are David's six stated criteria, one-to-one.

| ID | Criterion | when / then | verify.machine | verify.human (glass) |
|----|-----------|-------------|----------------|----------------------|
| ICL-C1 | No frozen gauge with no instance | WHEN no instance is connected THEN the pane shows the Launch affordance, NOT a stale gauge | a scene test publishes `console/link/state = channel_offline` and asserts `intelligence_panel` is absent from `_flow_children()` and contributes ZERO height, and `instance_launch_panel` is present | David kills the instance and watches the gauge be REPLACED (not dimmed) within ~12 s |
| ICL-C2 | The press spawns a real Terminal running the harness | WHEN the button is pressed THEN a Terminal window opens running `launch-instance.sh <id>` | selftest: the built argv is the `osascript`/`do script` form containing the repo-resolved script + validated id (spawn injected, no real window) | David presses it and a Terminal appears, RAISED, showing the plan preamble and then the real Claude Code UI |
| ICL-C3 | The pane swaps back with NO console bounce | WHEN `channel/alive` resumes THEN the gauge returns in the SAME console process | a test publishes `engaged` after `channel_offline` and asserts the shown-map flips back and the card re-enters the flow | David answers the dialogs and the gauge returns live in the console he never restarted |
| ICL-C4 | A second press does not spawn a second instance | WHEN the intent arrives twice while a launch is in flight THEN exactly ONE spawn occurs | [CXC-ICL-SPAWN-ONCE] legs (a)-(d) — including leg (d), which FAILS if the raw `osascript` handle is registered instead of the `LaunchHandle`. Satisfied by the DAEMON, not by a pane state, so the same test covers the bus path | David double-clicks; one Terminal |
| ICL-C5 | Control-is-a-peer: an Intelligence publish == a human press | WHEN an Intelligence publishes the identical intent THEN the outcome is identical, INCLUDING the dedupe | a test publishes `_app/intelligence/open` twice directly with no widget in the process and asserts one spawn + a `skipped` — the same assertion as ICL-C4 | David asks the Intelligence to publish it and watches the same Terminal open |
| ICL-C6 | Nothing regresses when an instance IS connected | WHEN an instance is connected THEN the pane behaves exactly as today | the console capture-diff (`python/tests/render/captures/`) is byte-identical for the connected/headless case ([CXC-ICL-PRESENCE] headless default + [CXC-ICL-PANE-SWAP] `initial:`) | David bounces the console beside a live instance and sees no visual change at all |
| ICL-C7 | ONE reachability computation, ONE dedupe mechanism | WHEN the guard tests run THEN no second freshness timer and no second dedupe exist | `DAEMONS_FRESH_S` defined once and shared by the panel and the launcher (asserted equal); the panel derives NO reachability verdict of its own; `app_launcher` holds exactly one `SpawnedWindows` | — |
| ICL-C8 | A live HARNESS is never superseded | WHEN the intent arrives for a HARNESSED id THEN nothing spawns and nothing is reaped | [CXC-ICL-PEER-GUARD]'s test | David publishes the intent while talking to a harnessed instance; the conversation is uninterrupted |
| ICL-C14 | The unharnessed instance is SURFACED, not hidden | WHEN `channel/alive` is fresh and `intelligence_launcher` is `down` THEN the pane warns and offers the launch | the four-way truth-table test ([CXC-ICL-PRESENCE]); plus a REGRESSION test that a reachability-only design FAILS it | David, on the bare session that raised this, sees the warning instead of a healthy gauge |
| ICL-C15 | A held lock fails LOUDLY, never silently | WHEN the flock is held and a launcher starts THEN no child spawns and the holder is named | [CXC-ICL-SINGLETON-YIELD]'s test, including the grep guard that `acquire_singleton_superseding` has no caller left in `launcher.py` | David reads the message in the Terminal and can act on it |
| ICL-C16 | A crash still never blocks a restart | WHEN a launcher dies THEN the next launch proceeds | a test that releases the flock by process death and asserts a clean subsequent acquire — the one property supersede bought, retained under yield | — |
| ICL-C9 | Injection-safe | WHEN a hostile id is published THEN it never reaches the shell | negative tests for `a; rm -rf /` and a path-traversal id | — |
| ICL-C10 | The register-guard still passes | WHEN the app_launcher selftest runs THEN every roster intent (now 8) has a consumer | `python3 intelligence/tools/app_launcher.py --selftest` green with the roster set updated | — |
| ICL-C11 | The whole loop, on the glass | WHEN the console is up with no instance THEN David reaches an ORIENTED, self-compacting Intelligence without typing a command | — (there is no machine signal for "the loop closed") | **David: press -> answer dialogs -> the instance comes up re-registered off its gifts ([CXC-ICL-COLD]) -> gauge live -> the instance emits a `/compact` directive and the launcher types it.** Mandatory, non-delegable |
| ICL-C12 | The bootstrap prose no longer contradicts the code | WHEN the grep guard runs THEN no site still calls the bootstrap experimental/default-off | a grep guard over `launcher.py` + `intelligence/launcher/README.md` ([CXC-ICL-COLD]) | — |
| ICL-C13 | Suites stay green | WHEN the slice lands THEN no test regresses | `pytest intelligence/` + the render suite green, no unexplained drop in collected count | — |
| ICL-C17 | `degraded` is COMPUTED — once, in `link_health`, under BOTH triggers ([DD-ICL-DEGRADED]) | WHEN `delivery != "ok"`, OR `unacked` climbs across `DEGRADED_CLIMB_N` consecutive payloads, THEN `console/link/state` publishes `degraded` | a `link_health` unit test drives each trigger INDEPENDENTLY under an injected clock and asserts the published verdict; plus the PRECEDENCE leg — a stale heartbeat while `delivery != "ok"` publishes `channel_offline`, NEVER `degraded` ([CXC-ICL-PRESENCE] cascade) | — |
| ICL-C18 | The hysteresis holds — one blip cannot flap the most-watched pixel | WHEN a single bad payload is followed by a clean one THEN the verdict does not oscillate | a test feeds bad->good->bad->good and asserts recovery requires `DEGRADED_RECOVER_N` clean payload(s) (`delivery == "ok"` AND non-increasing `unacked`); that any non-increasing `unacked` RESETS the climb run; that a missing `delivery` reads as `ok` and a missing `unacked` resets the run; and that a channel which STOPS publishing ages into `channel_offline` rather than recovering out of `degraded` by silence | — |
| ICL-C19 | ONE computation — and EVERY consumer actually renders it | WHEN `degraded` is published THEN no surface re-derives it and no surface falls back to its healthy default | (a) the panel module contains no read of `delivery`/`unacked` — the [CXC-ICL-PRESENCE] grep test, extended, so the pane stays DOWNSTREAM of the single verdict — and the only occurrence of the literal `degraded` in that module is its mapping to `reachable = yes` ([CXC-ICL-PANEL]); (b) a scene test enumerates EVERY attribute `map:` bound to `console/link/state` in `console.compose.yaml` and asserts each carries a `degraded` key — the omission that would render a degraded channel as a green ENGAGED header ([CXC-ICL-BOUNDARIES] row 17); (c) the truth-table test asserts `degraded` selects the SAME state as `engaged` for every harness value, so `intelligence_panel` stays shown, `instance_launch_panel` stays hidden, and the launch button is never revealed | — |
| ICL-C20 | The degraded condition is legible ON THE GLASS, on both surfaces — and the thresholds get settled | WHEN the channel degrades THEN David can tell, from the console alone, that messages may not be arriving | slice 5's capture re-baseline is reviewed on its OWN, containing the dot/label/marker change and NOTHING else — no rail reflow in the same diff ([CXC-ICL-SLICES] slice 5) | **David, on a genuinely degraded channel or with `degraded` injected, sees the header go amber and read `DEGRADED` and the gauge card carry its marker — and CONFIRMS the gauge did not vanish and the launch button did not appear. He settles the provisional thresholds here: too eager, too lax, or right ([CXC-ICL-PRESENCE] known-hazard).** Mandatory, non-delegable |

- constraint: ICL-C11 CANNOT be waived by a green machine run. Every mechanism in this spec can pass in
  isolation while the end-to-end loop fails for a reason only a human at the Terminal will see (a
  dialog that did not appear, a window that opened behind the console, an `.mcp.json` that did not
  register the channel, a bootstrap that landed before the dialogs were done). That is the whole reason
  the proxy is transparent.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

---

## 8. Sequencing

### [CXC-ICL-SLICES] Five slices, smallest blast radius first

- definition: each slice is independently landable and revertable and ends with the criteria that apply
  to it.

| Slice | Content | Why here | Revert |
|-------|---------|----------|--------|
| 1 | `launch-instance.sh` + the shared `.mcp.json` helper + the `--dry-run` preamble + the `BOOTSTRAP` default flip and the three prose corrections ([CXC-ICL-COLD]). No console change. | It is the piece a HUMAN can validate by hand before any button exists ([CXC-ICL-ENTRY] "usable by hand, identically"). If the script is wrong, everything above it is untestable. The prose fix rides here because it is the same file family. | delete the script; revert one default |
| 1b | The singleton flip to yield-if-held + loud ([CXC-ICL-SINGLETON-YIELD]) and the `transport.sh` re-framing ([CXC-ICL-CLI-UNSUPPORTED]). | Its own commit because it changes the behaviour of an EXISTING path used today, independently of anything else here, and it is strictly safer on that path (refuse-and-say beats reap-a-live-harness). Landing it early also means slices 2-3 are built against the final lock semantics. | one call site |
| 2 | `app_launcher`: the `intelligence` TARGET_TABLE row, the `osascript` `_terminal_argv` fix, `open(target, payload)`, id validation, the `LaunchHandle` + [CXC-ICL-SPAWN-ONCE], the `self/daemons` + `channel/alive` subscriptions + [CXC-ICL-PEER-GUARD]. Driven by a bus publish, no UI. | Proves ICL-C2/C4/C5/C8/C9 with NO console risk at all — the console is not touched. Control-is-a-peer means the whole feature is reachable from the bus before any pixel moves. | one file |
| 3 | The `instance_launch_panel` widget + the scene wiring (`visibility_namespace`, the `self/daemons` + `console/instance/pane` + `console/instance/harness` declarations, the rail child, the card's harness marker). | The only slice that can move a pixel in the console; it lands after the launch path is already proven, so a failure here is unambiguously a UI failure. Gated on the capture-diff (ICL-C6) with the marker re-baselined once. | revert the scene block + delete the widget |
| 4 | Glass run: ICL-C11 end to end, including a real `/compact` self-direction from the launched instance. | The loop is not closed until the launched instance can compact itself — that is the entire point of the harness. | n/a |
| 5 | `degraded` end to end ([DD-ICL-DEGRADED]): the THIRD verdict value + `DEGRADED_CLIMB_N` / `DEGRADED_RECOVER_N` + the precedence cascade in `link_health`; the `degraded` key added to EVERY `map:` bound to `console/link/state` (`link_label`, `composer_send_label`); and the `intelligence_card`'s `channel: degraded` marker. NO presence logic changes — the truth table is untouched ([CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY]). Ends with ICL-C17..C20. | Its correctness is NOT machine-verifiable in one pass: it changes the header dot's SEMANTICS *and* the pane, both human-perceptible, and it needs its own capture re-baseline. Folding it into slice 3 would make THAT slice's capture-diff unreadable — a rail reflow and a dot recolour in one diff is a diff nobody can read — which was the original deferral's stated reason and is still true. It lands AFTER slice 4 so the launch loop is already closed and the only thing moving in this diff is the delivery signal. | `link_health`'s cascade + the map entries + one bound label |
- constraint: slice 3 does not land without a CLEAN capture-diff. The console's failure mode is that it
  renders perfectly while the data is wrong; a pixel diff is the only instrument that sees a rail that
  silently reflowed. The same applies to slice 5, whose re-baseline MUST contain the dot/label/marker
  change and nothing else.
- rationale: **(why slice 5 is SPLIT — recorded so the split does not read as CAUTION)** this
  project's
  build doctrine is one pass, not small steps, and a slice is split when its CORRECTNESS cannot be
  established in one pass — never for size and never for nerves. Slice 5 qualifies on that test and on
  that test only: its verdict is two human-perceptible surface changes whose acceptance is a glass gate
  (ICL-C20) plus a capture re-baseline that has to stay READABLE. Slices 1-3 each have a machine gate
  that CLOSES them; slice 4 is itself a glass run, split for exactly this same reason and by the same
  rule; ICL-C20 has no machine gate that can close it either. That is the whole reason, written so
  nobody later "simplifies" the plan by folding 5 into 3 and loses the readable diff with it.
- dsd-source: **(slice 5 only)** docs/intelligence-console/DECISIONS-ic-launch-and-conops.md — slice
  5
  exists because David PROMOTED `degraded` from DEFERRED to shipping in DSD D2
  ([DEC-ICL-DEGRADED-CHANNEL-VISIBLE], [DD-ICL-DEGRADED]). Its SHAPE — the split, the rendering, the
  thresholds — is the composeux-architect's ratify layered on top of that call, not David's.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [CXC-ICL-OUT] Explicitly out of scope — named so it is not smuggled in

- out: auto-answering Claude Code's startup dialogs, by keystroke injection or any other means
  ([CXC-ICL-CONSTRAINT-PTY]). Permanently out, not deferred. (Distinct from the re-register BOOTSTRAP,
  which is now the default — see [CXC-ICL-COLD].)
- out: any change to the `/compact` actuation relay, its three-phase injection, or the harness monitor
  ([CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE], [CXC-IC-MONITOR]). This spec makes the harness EASIER TO START and touches
  nothing about what it does once started.
- out: RESUMING a specific prior session from the console, and any session picker. That stays the
- out: STOPPING or restarting a running instance from the console. This spec only ever adds an
  instance, never removes one ([CXC-ICL-PEER-GUARD]).
- out: the fleet roster UI and multi-instance cards ([CXC-IH-CONSOLE]) — though the per-id payload
  ([CXC-ICL-INTENT]) is deliberately shaped so the fleet case is an extension, not a rewrite.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

---

## 9. Design decisions

### [DD-ICL-COLD-VS-RESUME] Always cold with the bootstrap, vs resume-if-available

- decision: RESOLVED — always cold start, with the re-register bootstrap ON ([CXC-ICL-COLD]).
- ratified: David, 2026-08-10 — "The re-register bootstrap is not experimental any longer, it works
  quite well — it should be the default for new instances."
- rationale: the case for resume rested entirely on the premise that a cold successor is UNORIENTED,
  because its only orientation aid was documented experimental and collision-prone. That premise is
  false. With the bootstrap working, cold start is oriented, and it is also the only option that
  requires no heuristic: resume would have meant mirroring a third-party tool's project-dir path
  encoding and then guessing which `.jsonl` the human meant. One button, one predictable behaviour;
- alternatives: (ii) resume-if-available, cold otherwise, with the button label stating the plan —
  strictly more context preserved when it works, at the cost of a two-layer heuristic and a variable
  button; (iii) two affordances — most honest, but it puts a session picker into a 332px rail card to
  serve a choice the human is better placed to make deliberately.
- status: resolved
- invalidating_assumption: "That the re-register bootstrap reliably produces an oriented instance, AND
  that losing an exited session's intact context is acceptable because the gifts rebuild enough. If the
  bootstrap regresses (a multi-line prompt submitting early on an embedded newline is its known
  historical failure), or if David finds himself repeatedly re-hosting by hand right after pressing the
  button, alternative (ii) reopens — and the candidate rule it needs is exactly the one deleted here."
- documents: [CXC-ICL-COLD]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [DD-ICL-NO-LAUNCHING-STATE] The anti-double-spawn guard belongs in the daemon, not in a third pane state

- decision: RESOLVED — the pane has exactly two configurations ([CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY]); spawn-once lives
  in `app_launcher` via `spawned_windows` ([CXC-ICL-SPAWN-ONCE]).
- ratified: David, 2026-08-10 (the button is visible only when no instance is connected), with the
  refinement that the guard moves to the consumer.
- the-objection-this-answers: **(recorded because it is real)** between the press and the first
  `channel/alive` beat the instance is NOT connected, so the button is still on screen and still
  pressable — for as long as the human takes to answer the dialogs. A second press would spawn a second
  launcher, and the launcher's singleton SUPERSEDES, so it could reap the very instance whose dialogs
  the human was mid-way through answering. The window is real; the question was only WHERE to close it.
- rationale: closing it with a UI latch protects the human path and leaves the BUS path unguarded,
  because an Intelligence never consults the widget — the exact asymmetry control-is-a-peer exists to
  prevent. Closing it in the daemon gives a double-click and a double-publish the identical outcome,
  keeps the widget a pure intent emitter, and reuses a seam that already exists and is already shared
  ([DX-NARROW-WAIST]) instead of adding a state machine.
- alternatives: (a) a `launching` pane state with its own timeout — more visible, but it is UI-only
  protection plus a second timer that can disagree with the daemon; (b) both — belt and braces, but two
  mechanisms for one guarantee is the fork class.
- status: resolved
- invalidating_assumption: "That `spawned_windows`' duck-typed `poll()` contract stays duck-typed. The
  whole reuse rests on registering a `LaunchHandle` rather than the `osascript` `Popen`
  ([CXC-ICL-SPAWN-ONCE]). If `SpawnedWindows` is ever narrowed to real `subprocess.Popen` objects, this
  guard silently becomes a no-op — which is why leg (d) of its test asserts the failure mode directly."
- documents: [CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [DD-ICL-UNHARNESSED-ACTION] What the `unharnessed` pane should OFFER — warn-only vs launch-anyway vs launch under a DISTINCT recovery id

- decision: RESOLVED — alternative (c): the state-2 button is ENABLED and launches under a DISTINCT,
  RECOVERY-scoped id — `recovery-architect-2` for the default `architect` — and NEVER under the
  canonical id. The running bare session is left untouched; nothing is asked to exit.
- ratified: David, 2026-08-10 — "This scenario is a recovery/fallback mode when there are operational
  problems that preclude the normal use of the IC and a harnessed agent. Suggest using
  'recovery-architect-2' for clarity."
- dsd-source: docs/intelligence-console/DECISIONS-ic-launch-and-conops.md
- rationale: state 2 needs an ACTION, not a notice board — a dead-end warning sends the human to the
  CLI ritual this spec is retiring ([CXC-ICL-CLI-UNSUPPORTED]). The only action physically available is
  a SECOND instance, and the sole objection to that was the shared-id clobber. Renaming the second
  instance removes the objection entirely rather than trading it for a manual precondition: the human
  gets a working harnessed Intelligence immediately, the bare session keeps running untouched, and no
  namespace has two publishers at any point. It is the alternative with the action AND without the
  hazard, and it only looked expensive while the second instance was mis-framed as a rival architect.
- reframing: **(what David's call actually changed, and why it DISSOLVES (c)'s recorded cost)** this
  node
  had costed (c) as "no collision, but it fragments the identity and instance-keyed home
  ([CXC-IH-HOMES]) for what is meant to be a repair." That cost assumed the second instance was a PEER
  architect competing for the same identity. It is not. State 2 is a RECOVERY/FALLBACK mode — the
  normal use of the IC plus a harnessed agent is already precluded — and a `recovery-` prefixed
  instance never CLAIMS to BE the architect, so the which-one-is-canonical question does not arise and
  there is no identity to fragment. The rename PRESERVES the identity rather than splitting it; that is
  why the FRAMING, not the id string, is the load-bearing half of this ratify.
- requirement: **(the id rule, stated so it is not invented)** the recovery id is `recovery-<id>-2`,
  which
  for the default `architect` yields `recovery-architect-2`. It MUST satisfy the same
  `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}$` validation as any other id ([CXC-ICL-INTENT]), and it is what the panel
  puts in the intent payload in state 2 — the canonical id is NEVER published from state 2. It takes
  its own instance home `~/compose-instances/recovery-architect-2/` by the ordinary rule
  ([CXC-ICL-CWD]) with no special case, and it is cold-started with the bootstrap like any other
  ([CXC-ICL-COLD]).
- consequence: **(the clobber hazard is CLOSED BY CONSTRUCTION, not mitigated)** two instances can
  no
  longer share an id, so the `self/<id>/*` + `intelligence/<id>/*` clobber ([CXC-IH-5A]) cannot occur
  at all — row 15 of [CXC-ICL-BOUNDARIES] is rewritten from KNOWN HAZARD to CLOSED. The peer guard
  ([CXC-ICL-PEER-GUARD]) evaluates the RECOVERY id, which is unharnessed by definition, so the launch
  proceeds without the guard needing a state-2 exception.
- the-tension-this-replaces: **(kept for the record)** the console CANNOT re-host the running bare
  session —
  `claude --resume` requires it to have exited, and nothing on the bus can make a live instance quit
  (the channel pushes CONTENT a mind reads, not control keys; that asymmetry is [CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE]'s
  whole premise). Under the previous PROPOSED form the only action was a SECOND instance under the SAME
  id, which clobbered until the human exited the first. The rename removes the clobber without needing
  the human to exit anything, so the manual precondition disappears along with the hazard.
- alternatives: (a) WARN-ONLY — no button in state 2; strictly safe, no clobber possible, but it makes
  the pane a notice board and pushes the human off the supported path onto the CLI ritual this spec is
  retiring ([CXC-ICL-CLI-UNSUPPORTED]); (b) offer the launch and ALSO publish an advisory onto
  `intelligence/<id>/in` asking the running instance to exit — respects the content/control split, but
  it depends on a mind cooperating and is unbounded in time; (b2) the PROPOSED form this supersedes —
  launch under the CANONICAL id with the body instructing the human to "exit it first" — rejected: it
  traded a transient clobber AND a manual precondition for nothing the rename does not give for free.
- recorded-not-taken: **(raised by the architect; it COMPOSES with this decision rather than
  replacing it)** PREVENT the state rather than remediate it — have `channel_bridge` REFUSE the
  canonical id
  unless a harness is present for that id, auto-falling back to a distinct `architect-bare`. That would
  make "reachable but unharnessed under the canonical id" UNREACHABLE and retire state 2 entirely, so
  the pane would never need a recovery affordance at all. It is not taken here because it changes a
  SHARED transport component's behaviour, which is outside this spec's boundary, and because a fallback
  still needs somewhere to launch from. If it is ever built, THIS decision remains correct: the
  recovery launch becomes the path for the residual cases prevention does not cover, and state 2
  becomes rare rather than wrong.
- status: resolved
- invalidating_assumption: "That a recovery instance under a distinct id is USEFUL — that the gifts +
  bootstrap ([CXC-ICL-COLD]) orient `recovery-architect-2` well enough to perform the repair, and that
  no console surface (the gauge, the transcript pin, the composer's send target, a future fleet roster)
  hard-codes the canonical id and therefore renders the recovery instance INVISIBLE. If a recovery
  instance comes up unreachable from the console's own surfaces, this reopens as alternative (a) plus
  the prevention note above — a button that launches an instance nobody can see is worse than no
  button."
- documents: [CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [DD-ICL-SINGLETON-YIELD] Yield-if-held vs supersede vs console-owned lifecycle

- decision: RESOLVED — yield-if-held + LOUD ([CXC-ICL-SINGLETON-YIELD]).
- ratified: David, 2026-08-10 — CLI invocation of `transport.sh` ceases to be a supported path, so the
  uncoordinated-entry-points world supersede was defending against no longer exists.
- rationale: supersede's own justification is a comment about a SILENT restart failure in a world with
  several entry points. With one entry point the console refuses before spawning
  ([CXC-ICL-PEER-GUARD]), so a held lock is an anomaly, and the cure for a silent failure is
  VISIBILITY, not bulldozing. Yield retains the one property supersede genuinely bought — a crash
  releases the flock, so a crash never blocks a restart (ICL-C16).
- alternatives: (a) keep SUPERSEDE — no change, but it means any stray launch can reap the console's
  live child, which is the hazard [CXC-TERM-SINGLETON] independently flagged from the other direction;
  (b) CONSOLE-OWNED LIFECYCLE — rejected on a structural fact, not a preference: the launcher is a
  child of Terminal.app, never of `app_launcher` (which only ever holds a millisecond-lived `osascript`
  handle), so the console cannot reap it; (c) a console-specific singleton variant — rejected by
  [CXC-TERM-SINGLETON]'s own constraint that this must be a re-ratification of [CXC-IC-SINGLETON], not
  a local exception.
- status: resolved
- invalidating_assumption: "That the IC remains the SOLE entry point. If any second path to spawning a
  launcher is ever restored — a CLI ritual, a LaunchAgent, a remote trigger — the uncoordinated world
  returns, a held lock stops being an anomaly, and supersede's original argument becomes live again.
  The yield decision is downstream of the one-door decision and must be revisited with it."
- documents: [CXC-ICL-SINGLETON-YIELD]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [DD-ICL-INTENT-KEY] `_app/intelligence/open` (new key) vs re-purposing `_app/harness/open`

- decision: RESOLVED — the PROPOSED form stands: a NEW target key `intelligence`, so the console's
  launch button publishes `_app/intelligence/open`. `_app/harness/open` keeps its ratified FOCUS/HOST
  semantics entirely untouched.
- ratified: composeux-architect, 2026-08-10 — ratified by the ARCHITECT, not David, and recorded that
  way deliberately. David settled FOUR other calls in this spec through his Decision-Support-Doc
  (`docs/intelligence-console/DECISIONS-ic-launch-and-conops.md`); this was NOT one of them, and a
  reader must not misattribute it to him. It is the architect's ratify because the spec's own argument
  is decisive and the only alternative breaks a semantic David had ALREADY ratified — a call whose
  second option is self-refuting does not need his attention, but it does need an honest record of
  whose call it was.
- rationale: `_app/harness/open` was RATIFIED as a FOCUS, not a spawn (David 2026-07-29,
  [DD-LB-LAUNCH-MECHANISM], recorded in `TARGET_TABLE`'s own comment: "the AI harness tile FOCUSES the
  already-running harness, it does NOT spawn a new PTY session"), and it is what the Launch Bar's tile
  2 publishes ([CXC-LB-ROSTER]). Re-purposing it would mean the SAME key means focus-an-existing-window
  when pressed on the ribbon and spawn-a-new-instance when pressed in the console — two behaviours
  behind one name, AT THE CONTRACT LAYER, which makes the Launch Bar's behaviour depend on WHICH
  SURFACE pressed it. That is not a naming preference; it is
  [INSIGHT-FORK-DIVERGENCE-IS-A-CLASS] created deliberately. A new key costs ONE table row in
  `app_launcher.py` ([CXC-ICL-TERMINAL]; no second daemon, [CXC-ICL-SCOPE-BOUNDARY]) and keeps FOCUS
  and SPAWN separately evolvable. The `open` suffix is forced by the existing
  `composeio/global/_app/+/open` subscription ([CXC-ICL-INTENT]).
- consequence: with this closed, [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH] has ZERO open decision points — every `DD-ICL-`
  decision node in section 9 carries `status: resolved`.
- alternatives: (a) re-purpose `_app/harness/open` — fewer keys, but it inverts a ratified decision and
  conflates FOCUS with INSTANTIATE on a tile that already ships; (b) `_app/harness/launch` — reads
  better but is NOT RECEIVED by the existing wildcard and would force a second subscription or a second
  daemon, i.e. the fork [DD-LB-LAUNCH-MECHANISM] rejected.
- status: resolved
- invalidating_assumption: "That FOCUS and SPAWN stay genuinely DISTINCT operations. If the launcher
  ever gains focus-or-spawn-as-appropriate semantics — press once to spawn, press again to focus what
  you spawned — the two keys COLLAPSE into one and `harness` should become a kind that dispatches on
  liveness, which is exactly [CXC-ICL-PEER-GUARD]'s check, already built. That revisit MUST happen
  TOGETHER WITH [DD-LB-LAUNCH-MECHANISM] and never independently: this decision is downstream of the
  tile-2-is-a-FOCUS decision, and re-opening one without the other would leave the corpus asserting
  both at once."
- documents: [CXC-ICL-INTENT]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [DD-ICL-LAUNCH-HINT] A purely COSMETIC "launch requested" sub-line during the launch window

- decision: RESOLVED — YES, as recommended: the panel's sub-line reads "launch requested — answer the
  dialogs in the Terminal window" for `LAUNCH_HINT_S` (90 s) after it last published an intent
  ([CXC-ICL-PANEL]). It SHIPS with the panel in slice 3; it is not a follow-up and it is not optional.
- ratified: David, 2026-08-10 — take the recommended YES. An unresponsive-looking button is the thing
  humans press again, and a label that changes no behaviour is the cheapest possible answer to that
  acknowledgement gap.
- dsd-source: docs/intelligence-console/DECISIONS-ic-launch-and-conops.md
- rationale: with no `launching` state, the pane is visually IDENTICAL before and after a press, for as
  long as the human takes at the dialogs. That is not a correctness problem — the daemon dedupes — but
  it is an acknowledgement problem: an unresponsive-looking button is the thing humans press again, and
  it is the same class as the silent-staleness this spec exists to fix, just pointed at the human
  instead of the data. A label costs nothing and answers "did that register?".
- constraint: **(what makes it safe — this is the CONDITION of the ratify and is preserved
  VERBATIM)** it
  changes NO behaviour. The button stays enabled, a press still publishes, the shown-map is untouched,
  and nothing depends on the timer. If it is ever found to gate anything, it has become a state and
  must be re-ratified as one.
- alternatives: (a) nothing at all — David's earlier minimal form, and defensible: the Terminal window
  appearing IS the acknowledgement, and it appears within a second. NOT taken; (b) a disabled button —
  rejected, that is the third state under another name and it would also disable the legitimate retry
  path row 9 of [CXC-ICL-BOUNDARIES] depends on.
- status: resolved
- invalidating_assumption: "That the Terminal window is not itself sufficient acknowledgement. If it
  reliably appears and RAISES within ~1 s ([CXC-ICL-TERMINAL] `activate`), the hint is redundant and
  should be DROPPED rather than maintained — dropping it is a no-behaviour-change removal, exactly as
  adding it was. The second and sharper trigger: any code that consults `LAUNCH_HINT_S` for anything
  other than choosing the sub-line's TEXT has turned the hint into a state under another name, and the
  constraint above requires that it be re-ratified as one."
- documents: [CXC-ICL-PANEL]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [DD-ICL-DEGRADED] Whether `delivery != "ok"` / rising `unacked` becomes a visible condition

- decision: RESOLVED — alternative (a): `degraded` is added to BOTH the header dot AND the pane. The
  DEFERRAL is reversed; it ships as part of this arc, not after it.
- ratified: David, 2026-08-10 — surface it. His call FALSIFIES this node's own deferral premise: a
  degraded channel is NOT rare enough to leave unsurfaced, and "messages look sent but the Intelligence
  never sees them" behind a green dot is the same silent-staleness class this whole spec exists to
  remove — one layer deeper than the frozen gauge, and correspondingly less visible.
- dsd-source: docs/intelligence-console/DECISIONS-ic-launch-and-conops.md
- rationale: the signal is FREE — the alive payload already carries `delivery` and `unacked`, and the
  console already computes and publishes a verdict over that payload. What the deferral bought was a
  clean capture-diff for the swap slice; what it cost was leaving the console's most-trusted pixel able
  to read GREEN while content is not arriving. That is the same silent-staleness failure as the frozen
  gauge, and this spec cannot claim to be an honesty fix while shipping a known instance of it. The
  sequencing cost is real but payable (its own commit, its own re-baseline, its own glass gate); the
  dishonesty is not.
- constraint: **(BINDING — the pane stays DOWNSTREAM of the single verdict)** `degraded` is a THIRD
  VALUE
  of `link_health`'s existing verdict, NOT a second computation. `link_health` reads `delivery` and
  `unacked` from the alive payload, folds them into the ONE verdict it already publishes to
  `console/link/state`, and BOTH consumers — the header dot and the launch panel — read that one value
  ([CXC-ICL-PRESENCE]'s ONE-computation rule, unchanged). The pane MUST NOT derive `degraded`
  independently from the alive payload: two derivations of one fact is the fork [DX-NARROW-WAIST]
  forbids, and here it has a specific, ugly failure — the dot and the pane disagree, and a human has no
  way to tell which one is lying.
- consequence: **(the deferral's stated cost is ACCEPTED, not dodged)** this DOES change the header
  dot's
  semantics and it DOES land on the visual-fidelity surface, which is exactly why it was deferred. It
  therefore carries its own glass gate and its own capture re-baseline, and it MUST NOT be folded
  silently into the swap slice's capture-diff ([CXC-ICL-SLICES] slice 3, [CXC-ICL-ACCEPT] ICL-C6) —
  a re-baseline that mixes the rail reflow with a dot recolour is a diff nobody can read.
- refinement: **(composeux-architect, 2026-08-10 — the ratify that makes David's call BUILDABLE)**
  his DSD
  D2 settled WHETHER; it stated no slice, no rendering and no threshold, and [CXC-ICL-SLICES] still
  carried four slices with none of them holding this work, so a builder would have had to guess. Closed
  here, on the architect's ratify: (i) `degraded` gets its OWN slice — [CXC-ICL-SLICES] slice 5 — on
  the one-pass test (its correctness is not machine-verifiable in one pass), NOT on size or caution;
  (ii) it resolves as `reachable = yes`, so it never withholds the gauge and NEVER reveals the launch
  button, and it renders as a MARKER on the shown surface with no fifth row in [CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY]
  ([CXC-ICL-PRESENCE]); (iii) the triggers, the hysteresis and the rendering values are stated
  concretely and marked PROVISIONAL pending David's glass, with ICL-C20 as the gate that settles them;
  (iv) a consequence neither this node nor the DSD had noticed — `console/link/state` has bound
  consumers BEYOND the dot and the pane (`link_label`, `composer_send_label`), and an attribute `map:`
  missing a `degraded` key falls back to its HEALTHY default, so a third verdict value shipped without
  those entries renders a degraded channel as a green ENGAGED header: the very lie this decision exists
  to remove, reintroduced by omission. ICL-C19 leg (b) is the guard.
- alternatives: (b) a text line in the pane without a verdict change — rejected: it still requires
  reading `delivery`, and it puts the fact on ONE surface while the dot keeps saying green, which is
  precisely the disagreement the constraint above exists to prevent; (c) stay DEFERRED — rejected by
  David.
- status: resolved
- invalidating_assumption: "That `delivery` and `unacked` are a TRUSTWORTHY degradation signal — that
  `delivery != ok` or a climbing `unacked` means content is genuinely not arriving, rather than a
  transient artifact of the channel's own ack accounting. If `degraded` is ever observed FLAPPING on a
  channel that is in fact delivering, it becomes a false alarm on the most-watched pixel in the
  console, and the correct retreat is alternative (b) — a text line — NOT a quietly widened threshold.
  The thresholds themselves are now STATED rather than deferred ([CXC-ICL-PRESENCE] defaults:
  `DEGRADED_CLIMB_N` 3, `DEGRADED_RECOVER_N` 1, plus the precedence cascade), but they are PROVISIONAL
  — defensible, not measured — and they remain the FIRST thing to revisit, on the glass, under
  [CXC-ICL-ACCEPT] ICL-C20."
- documents: [CXC-ICL-PRESENCE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

### [DD-ICL-PANE-OWNER] The launch panel owns the shown-map, vs a separate presence publisher

- decision: RESOLVED — the PROPOSED form stands unchanged: the `instance_launch_panel` widget is the
  SINGLE writer of `console/instance/pane` ([CXC-ICL-PANEL]).
- ratified: composeux-architect, 2026-08-10 — ratified by the ARCHITECT, not David, and deliberately
  so: this was never a genuine fork. Both alternatives are self-rejected in this node's OWN text — (a)
  is a whole registered data source, a singleton and a `run()`-must-block obligation
  ([CXC-SOB-RUN-BLOCKS]) for ONE boolean, and (b) overloads `link_health` with rail-layout knowledge, a
  layering inversion. A choice with no defensible second option does not need David's attention; what
  it needs is a record of WHY it did not, so it cannot later be re-opened as if it had been one.
- dsd-source: docs/intelligence-console/DECISIONS-ic-launch-and-conops.md
- rationale: it already needs the predicate to render itself, so the derivation exists in exactly one
  place and cannot disagree with what is drawn; it needs no new data source, no new process, and no new
  registration in `data_sources.py`; and it hides ITSELF, which keeps the reversibility of
  [SPEC:CXC-ORDER] intact. It is [DX-LEAF-OWNS-BEHAVIOR] applied literally.
- alternatives: (a) a new in-process data source that publishes the shown-map — a whole registered
  source, a singleton, a `run()`-must-block obligation ([CXC-SOB-RUN-BLOCKS]) for one boolean; (b)
  extending `link_health` to publish the shown-map too — overloads the header dot with rail-layout
  knowledge, a layering inversion.
- status: resolved
- invalidating_assumption: "That exactly one surface swaps on instance presence. If a second surface
  (the Account card, the composer's SEND button, a fleet rail) ever needs the same presence-derived
  shown-set, the derivation should move OUT of this widget into one publisher and every consumer should
  subscribe it — the same de-fork move [CXC-ICL-PRESENCE] already applies to the freshness computation."
- documents: [CXC-ICL-PANEL]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]

